//! Zip archives as values: read, written, and planned for extraction, without a file system.
//!
//! A zip archive is read from its end. `locate` finds the end record in an archive's last bytes and
//! returns where the directory is. `listing` reads the directory's headers. `open` reads one
//! member's own header from the bytes at its offset and returns where its data is, and `unpack`
//! turns that data into the member's bytes. Each of the four takes the bytes it needs, not a file
//! handle. `locate` and `open` return `NeedMore` with how many bytes they need when given fewer, so
//! a driver that reads a file at offsets can fetch one member without loading the archive into
//! memory or inflating any other member. `members` and `read` run those steps over a whole
//! archive's bytes.
//!
//! It reads stored and deflated members, zip64 in every record that has a zip64 form, and data
//! descriptors in all four of their forms. A member's time is an MS-DOS date and time: the wall
//! clock of whoever wrote it, with no time zone and so no instant. The extended timestamp field and
//! the NTFS field store instants, which are read when present. A Unix mode states what a member is
//! and its permissions, and any name that ends in `/` is also a directory. A header's other flags
//! and its version numbers are ignored.
//!
//! A name is UTF-8 when its header's flag says so, and is taken from the Info-ZIP Unicode path
//! field when one exists for the header's name. The format declares every other name to be code
//! page 437, yet the two most common writers, macOS's `zip` and Finder, write UTF-8 there anyway.
//! So a reader chooses with the `NameEncoding` it is given. `Utf8IfValid`, the default, reads such
//! a name as UTF-8 when its bytes are valid UTF-8 and as code page 437 otherwise. `Declared` reads
//! it as the format specifies and never guesses.
//!
//! The default misreads a code page 437 name that is also valid UTF-8. A name written in that code
//! page almost never is, because each of its non-ASCII characters would have to be a box-drawing,
//! Greek or mathematical character immediately followed by one to three accented letters or
//! symbols.
//!
//! `DirectoryEntry.name` and `Member.name` store the raw bytes under either rule, and a member's
//! comment is read by the same rule as its name. The two readings agree on every ASCII character,
//! so a path has the same `/`, `.`, `\` and `:` in the same places under both, and the checks that
//! depend only on those characters give the same result under either reading. They can decode a
//! non-ASCII name to different characters, so a plan is made, and checked, over the names as they
//! were decoded.
//!
//! An archive that contradicts itself is rejected, since two readers may each believe a different
//! version. A member's own header must agree with what the directory says of it: its name, its
//! flags, its method, and its checksum and sizes if it contains them, with the data descriptor
//! after its data if it does not. Two members may not have the same name or overlapping byte
//! ranges, a member may not overlap the directory, and the directory ends where its end record
//! begins. One end record ends the bytes: more bytes after it, or its signature again within its
//! comment, is rejected. The bytes before the directory that no member claims, such as a stub
//! before the first member or a signing block after the last, are limited to `Limits.slack`. With a
//! `slack` of zero, the members must follow one another with no gap and no extra bytes. Any method
//! but stored and deflated is rejected by its number, and so is any encryption.
//!
//! A reader takes its `Limits` as an argument: how many entries, how many bytes one entry and all
//! entries may unpack to, how many times its stored size one entry may unpack to, how long a name
//! and a comment may be, and the slack. They are checked against what the directory states, before
//! anything is unpacked. A member is then inflated to the stated size and no further, and the
//! output must be that long and match the directory's CRC-32.
//!
//! `write` emits the entries in order, each deflated when that is smaller and stored otherwise. It
//! writes complete headers and no data descriptor, the UTF-8 flag when a name or a comment is not
//! ASCII, a zip64 field exactly where a number overflows its header's field, and no extra field the
//! entry does not request. It writes nothing its input does not contain: `file`, `directory` and
//! `symlink` make entries modified at the start of 1980 unless given another time, so equal entries
//! produce equal bytes. `begin`, `add` and `finish` are `write` one member at a time, and `flush`
//! returns what is written so far, for a driver that does not keep it in memory. It reads back what
//! it writes, and it rejects what its reader would: a second entry with the same name, and bytes a
//! reader could mistake for another end record.
//!
//! An entry's path is whatever the archive says. `plan` returns the operations that extract a list
//! of entries beneath a root, or the entry that may not be extracted and why. It is `std.tar`'s
//! plan, with the same rules and the same `Operation`, `UnsafeEntry` and `UnsafeReason` types. It
//! also rejects a path or a link with a backslash or a drive letter, which the systems zip came
//! from read as a different location. A symbolic link's target is its data, and a FIFO, a device or
//! a socket is read as an `Other` that no plan creates. `tar::extract` carries out a plan beneath a
//! directory of a root.
//!
//! Not interpreted: owners and the times Finder writes (Info-ZIP's Unix fields), and every other
//! extra field. `Header.extra` stores them as written.

import std.bytes (u32_le, u64_le)
import std.checksum (crc32)
import std.date
import std.date (Date, DateTime, Time)
import std.fs
import std.gzip (deflate, try_inflate)
import std.list (enumerate)
import std.option
import std.result
import std.string
import std.tar
import std.tar (tar, Absolute, Backslash, UnsafeReason, Drive, Escapes, LinkEscapes, MakeDirectory,
                MakeSymlink, NoName, Operation, Special, ThroughLink, UnsafeEntry, WriteFile)
import std.time (nanos_at)
import std.gen

/// What an entry is.
pub type Kind =
  | File
  | Directory
  /// A symbolic link to the text in its data.
  | Symlink
  /// Anything else a Unix mode names, by its type bits: 1 a FIFO, 2 a character device, 6 a
  /// block device, 12 a socket.
  | Other(Int)

/// One extra field of a member's header: its id and its data.
pub type Field = new { id: Int, data: Bytes }

/// The access, creation and modification instants an archive can record for a member, in addition
/// to its wall-clock `modified` time. Each is `None` unless the archive recorded it.
pub type MemberTimes = new {
  modified: Option<Instant>,
  accessed: Option<Instant>,
  created: Option<Instant>,
}

/// The metadata an archive's directory records for one member, excluding the member's data.
pub type Header = new {
  /// The member's path exactly as the archive stores it, except that a directory's trailing `/` is
  /// removed. The name is decoded as the reader's `NameEncoding` directs unless the archive flags
  /// it as UTF-8. The path is not checked for safety; call `plan` to check it before extracting.
  path: String,
  kind: Kind,
  /// The Unix permission, set-id and sticky bits. An archive written on a Unix system stores them.
  /// An archive from any other system stores none, so the mode is `0755` for a directory and `0644`
  /// for anything else. If the member is marked read-only, the write bits are cleared.
  mode: Int,
  /// The modification time as the archive's MS-DOS date and time, which is a wall-clock time with
  /// no time zone, so it does not identify an instant. It has two-second precision and ranges from
  /// 1980 to 2107. It is `None` if the stored fields are not a valid date.
  modified: Option<DateTime>,
  /// The modification, access and creation instants. An extended timestamp field stores the
  /// modification time to the second. An NTFS field stores all three instants to a tenth of a
  /// microsecond. If both fields are present, the NTFS field is used.
  times: MemberTimes,
  comment: String,
  /// The extra fields that the other `Header` fields do not decode, such as the owner's user and
  /// group or a vendor's custom fields, in the order they appear in the directory.
  extra: List<Field>,
}

/// A member of an archive: its header and its data, which is a symbolic link's target.
pub type Entry = new { header: Header, data: Bytes }

fn header(path: String, kind: Kind, mode: Int, modified: DateTime) -> Header =
  {
    path: path,
    kind: kind,
    mode: mode,
    modified: Some(modified),
    times: { modified: None, accessed: None, created: None },
    comment: "",
    extra: [],
  }

/// A file's entry, mode `0644`, modified at the start of 1980: the earliest time a header can
/// store.
pub fn file(
  path: String,
  data: Bytes,
  mode: Int = 420,
  modified: DateTime = DateTime(Date(3652), Time(0)),
) -> Entry = { header: header(path, File, mode, modified), data: data }

/// A directory's entry, mode `0755`.
pub fn directory(
  path: String,
  mode: Int = 493,
  modified: DateTime = DateTime(Date(3652), Time(0)),
) -> Entry = { header: header(path, Directory, mode, modified), data: b"" }

/// A symbolic link's entry: `path` points at the text `target`, which is the entry's data.
pub fn symlink(
  path: String,
  target: String,
  modified: DateTime = DateTime(Date(3652), Time(0)),
) -> Entry = { header: header(path, Symlink, 511, modified), data: Bytes::of_string(target) }

/// Why a reader rejected an archive, or a writer an entry.
pub type ErrorKind =
  /// The bytes given end before the record they should contain.
  | Truncated
  /// The bytes contain no end record. They are not an archive, they stop before the archive ends,
  /// extra bytes follow the archive, or the archive's comment is longer than the reader's limit.
  | NoEndRecord
  /// The bytes contain more than one candidate end record. Either the end record's signature also
  /// appears inside the archive's comment, or the comments of two candidate end records both extend
  /// to the last byte.
  | ManyEndRecords
  /// One part of an archive split across several disks.
  | Split
  /// A directory that does not end where its end record begins, or does not contain the headers
  /// it counts.
  | BadDirectory
  /// A zip64 field or record that is missing where a marker or a locator says one is.
  | BadZip64
  /// A size, an offset or a count past an `Int`, a stored member whose two sizes differ, or a
  /// mode past `07777`.
  | BadNumber
  /// Extra data that is not a list of fields, each an id, a length and that many bytes; a field
  /// this module reads that appears twice, or is too short for what it claims to contain; or more
  /// fields than a header can store.
  | BadExtra
  /// Two records that should agree do not: a member's own header and the directory's header for
  /// it, the data descriptor after its data, or the end record and its zip64 record.
  | Disagrees
  /// Two members with the same name.
  | Duplicate
  /// A member whose bytes run into the next member's bytes or into the directory, or two members
  /// that start at the same offset.
  | Overlaps
  /// Bytes before the directory that no member claims, more than the reader's limit.
  | Unclaimed
  /// A member compressed by a method other than stored and deflated: its number.
  | Method(Int)
  /// An encrypted member, or a directory whose headers are masked.
  | Encrypted
  /// Text that is not valid UTF-8 where UTF-8 is required: a name or comment that the archive flags
  /// as UTF-8, a Unicode path field, or a link's target.
  | NotUtf8
  /// A name that ends in `/` on a member whose mode is not a directory's, a directory's mode on a
  /// name that does not end in `/`, or an `Other` whose number belongs to another kind.
  | BadKind
  /// Data on a directory.
  | StrayData
  /// More entries than the reader's limit.
  | TooMany
  /// An entry larger than the reader's limit.
  | TooLarge
  /// More data in all than the reader's limit.
  | TooMuch
  /// A name or a comment longer than the reader's limit, or than a header can store.
  | TooLong
  /// A member whose unpacked size divided by its stored size exceeds the reader's limit on that
  /// ratio.
  | TooDense
  /// Data that does not inflate, or inflates to a size other than the directory states.
  | BadData
  /// Data whose CRC-32 does not match the directory's.
  | BadChecksum
  /// A path a header cannot store: an empty one, one with a NUL, one that ends in `/`.
  | BadName
  /// A time a header cannot store: a wall clock before 1980, after 2107 or at an odd second,
  /// or an instant finer than a tenth of a microsecond.
  | BadTime

/// Why a reader rejected an archive, and where, as an offset into the bytes. The offset is that of
/// the rejected record, of the member whose bytes or data were rejected, or of bytes that lie
/// outside every member. When no end record was found, it is the offset of the end of the bytes.
pub type Error = new { at: Int, reason: ErrorKind }

/// Why a writer rejected an entry, and the index of that entry among those it was given. The
/// index is the number of entries when the archive's own comment was rejected.
pub type WriteError = new { index: Int, reason: ErrorKind }

/// The effect this module raises its errors through. Unsafe entries found while planning an
/// extraction are raised as `tar.unsafe` from `std.tar`, not through this effect.
pub effect zip {
  /// The bytes are not an archive within the limits.
  raise fail(e: Error)
  /// An entry, or the archive's comment, cannot be written.
  raise write_failed(e: WriteError)
}

fn fail<a>(at: Int, reason: ErrorKind) -> a / {zip.fail} = zip.fail({ at: at, reason: reason })

fn fail_write<a>(index: Int, reason: ErrorKind) -> a / {zip.write_failed} =
  zip.write_failed({ index: index, reason: reason })

fn u16(b: Bytes, at: Int) -> Int / {abort.raise} = b.at(at) | b.at(at + 1) << 8

fn u16_le(n: Int) -> Bytes = byte_of_int(n & 0xFF).concat(byte_of_int(n >>> 8 & 0xFF))

const zip64_marker: Int = 0xFFFF_FFFF

fn fields(block: Bytes) -> { found: List<Field>, whole: Bool } / {abort.raise} =
  fields_from(block, 0, [], block.len() / 4 + 1)

fn fields_from(
  block: Bytes,
  i: Int,
  accumulator: List<Field>,
  span: Int,
) -> { found: List<Field>, whole: Bool } / {abort.raise} = {
  let left = block.len() - i;
  if left == 0 { { found: accumulator, whole: true } }
  else if span <= 0 || left < 4 || u16(block, i + 2) > left - 4 {
    { found: accumulator, whole: false }
  } else {
    let size = u16(block, i + 2);
    fields_from(
      block,
      i + 4 + size,
      accumulator.push({ id: u16(block, i), data: block.slice(i + 4, i + 4 + size) }),
      span - 1,
    )
  }
}

fn field(extra: List<Field>, id: Int) -> Option<Bytes> =
  extra.find(|f: Field| f.id == id).map(|f: Field| f.data)

fn field_bytes(id: Int, data: Bytes) -> Bytes =
  Bytes::concat_all([u16_le(id), u16_le(data.len()), data])

fn is_known_field(id: Int) -> Bool = id == 0x0001 || id == 0x000A || id == 0x5455 || id == 0x7075

type HeaderNumbers = { size: Int, stored: Int, at: Int, disk: Int }

fn zip64_number(where: Int, data: Bytes, at: Int) -> Int / {zip.fail} =
  if data.len() - at < 8 { fail(where, BadZip64) }
  else { match data.u64_at(at) { Some(n) -> n, None -> fail(where, BadNumber) } }

fn with_zip64(
  where: Int,
  extra: List<Field>,
  narrow: HeaderNumbers,
  is_local_header: Bool,
) -> HeaderNumbers / {zip.fail} = {
  let sized = narrow.size == zip64_marker;
  let packed = narrow.stored == zip64_marker;
  let placed = narrow.at == zip64_marker;
  let spanned = narrow.disk == 0xFFFF;
  if !(sized || packed || placed || spanned) { narrow } else {
    match field(extra, 1) {
      None -> fail(where, BadZip64),
      Some(data) -> {
        let second_entry_error = if sized || is_local_header { 8 } else { 0 };
        let third = second_entry_error + (if packed { 8 } else { 0 });
        let fourth = third + (if placed { 8 } else { 0 });
        {
          size: if sized { zip64_number(where, data, 0) } else { narrow.size },
          stored: if packed { zip64_number(where, data, second_entry_error) }
          else { narrow.stored },
          at: if placed { zip64_number(where, data, third) } else { narrow.at },
          disk: if !spanned { narrow.disk }
          else { match data.u32_at(fourth) { Some(disk) -> disk, None -> fail(where, BadZip64) } },
        }
      },
    }
  }
}

/// How a reader decodes a name, or a member's comment, that its header does not flag as UTF-8
/// and no Unicode path field provides.
pub type NameEncoding =
  /// Decodes the bytes as code page 437, which is what the zip format specifies.
  | Declared
  /// As UTF-8 when its bytes are valid UTF-8, and as code page 437 otherwise. ASCII reads the
  /// same either way, and bytes that would be UTF-8 only through an overlong form or a surrogate
  /// are not valid UTF-8.
  | Utf8IfValid

const code_page_437: Array<Int> =
  Array::of_list(
    [
      [0x00C7, 0x00FC, 0x00E9, 0x00E2, 0x00E4, 0x00E0, 0x00E5, 0x00E7],
      [0x00EA, 0x00EB, 0x00E8, 0x00EF, 0x00EE, 0x00EC, 0x00C4, 0x00C5],
      [0x00C9, 0x00E6, 0x00C6, 0x00F4, 0x00F6, 0x00F2, 0x00FB, 0x00F9],
      [0x00FF, 0x00D6, 0x00DC, 0x00A2, 0x00A3, 0x00A5, 0x20A7, 0x0192],
      [0x00E1, 0x00ED, 0x00F3, 0x00FA, 0x00F1, 0x00D1, 0x00AA, 0x00BA],
      [0x00BF, 0x2310, 0x00AC, 0x00BD, 0x00BC, 0x00A1, 0x00AB, 0x00BB],
      [0x2591, 0x2592, 0x2593, 0x2502, 0x2524, 0x2561, 0x2562, 0x2556],
      [0x2555, 0x2563, 0x2551, 0x2557, 0x255D, 0x255C, 0x255B, 0x2510],
      [0x2514, 0x2534, 0x252C, 0x251C, 0x2500, 0x253C, 0x255E, 0x255F],
      [0x255A, 0x2554, 0x2569, 0x2566, 0x2560, 0x2550, 0x256C, 0x2567],
      [0x2568, 0x2564, 0x2565, 0x2559, 0x2558, 0x2552, 0x2553, 0x256B],
      [0x256A, 0x2518, 0x250C, 0x2588, 0x2584, 0x258C, 0x2590, 0x2580],
      [0x03B1, 0x00DF, 0x0393, 0x03C0, 0x03A3, 0x03C3, 0x00B5, 0x03C4],
      [0x03A6, 0x0398, 0x03A9, 0x03B4, 0x221E, 0x03C6, 0x03B5, 0x2229],
      [0x2261, 0x00B1, 0x2265, 0x2264, 0x2320, 0x2321, 0x00F7, 0x2248],
      [0x00B0, 0x2219, 0x00B7, 0x221A, 0x207F, 0x00B2, 0x25A0, 0x00A0],
    ].flatten(
    ),
  )

fn of_code_page_437(raw: Bytes) -> String / {abort.raise} =
  if raw.is_utf8() && String::of_bytes(raw).is_ascii() { String::of_bytes(raw) } else {
    let high = code_page_437;
    String::of_chars(
      range(0, raw.len()).map(|i: Int| {
        let byte = raw.at(i);
        match Char::of_int(if byte < 0x80 { byte } else { high.get(byte - 0x80) }) {
          Some(c) -> c,
          None -> '\u{fffd}',
        }
      }),
    )
  }

fn utf8(where: Int, raw: Bytes) -> String / {zip.fail, abort.raise} =
  if raw.is_utf8() { String::of_bytes(raw) } else { fail(where, NotUtf8) }

fn decode_unflagged(raw: Bytes, names: NameEncoding) -> String / {abort.raise} =
  if names == Utf8IfValid && raw.is_utf8() { String::of_bytes(raw) } else { of_code_page_437(raw) }

fn from_dos_time(day: Int, time: Int) -> Option<DateTime> =
  date::date_time(
    1980 + (day >> 9),
    day >> 5 & 15,
    day & 31,
    time >> 11,
    time >> 5 & 63,
    (time & 31) * 2,
  )

fn to_dos_time(at: DateTime) -> Option<Int> = {
  let day = date::parts(date::date_of(at));
  let time = date::time_of(at);
  let second_entry_error = date::second(time);
  if day.year < 1980 || day.year > 2107 || second_entry_error % 2 != 0
      || date::nanosecond(time) != 0 { None } else {
    Some(
      (day.year - 1980 << 9 | day.month << 5 | day.day) << 16 | date::hour(time) << 11
        | date::minute(time) << 5
        | second_entry_error / 2,
    )
  }
}

fn dos_time_or_zero(modified: Option<DateTime>) -> Option<Int> =
  match modified { Some(at) -> to_dos_time(at), None -> Some(0) }

const ntfs_epoch: Int = 116444736000000000

fn of_ticks(ticks: Option<Int>) -> Option<Instant> =
  match ticks {
    Some(n) -> if n == 0 { None }
    else { checked_mul(n - ntfs_epoch, 100).map(|nanos: Int| Instant(nanos)) },
    None -> None,
  }

fn ticks_of(at: Option<Instant>) -> Option<Int> =
  match at {
    None -> Some(0),
    Some(instant) -> {
      let nanos = nanos_at(instant);
      if nanos / 100 * 100 != nanos { None } else { Some(nanos / 100 + ntfs_epoch) }
    },
  }

const no_times: MemberTimes = { modified: None, accessed: None, created: None }

fn whole_seconds(at: Option<Instant>) -> Option<Int> =
  match at {
    None -> None,
    Some(instant) -> {
      let seconds = nanos_at(instant) / 1000000000;
      if seconds * 1000000000 != nanos_at(instant) || seconds < -0x8000_0000
          || seconds >= 0x8000_0000 { None }
      else { Some(seconds) }
    },
  }

fn ntfs_times(where: Int, data: Bytes) -> MemberTimes / {zip.fail, abort.raise} =
  if data.len() < 4 { fail(where, BadExtra) } else {
    let attributes = fields(data.slice(4, data.len()));
    match field(attributes.found, 1) {
      None -> if attributes.whole { no_times } else { fail(where, BadExtra) },
      Some(times) -> if !attributes.whole || times.len() < 24 { fail(where, BadExtra) } else {
        {
          modified: of_ticks(times.u64_at(0)),
          accessed: of_ticks(times.u64_at(8)),
          created: of_ticks(times.u64_at(16)),
        }
      },
    }
  }

fn times_of(where: Int, extra: List<Field>) -> MemberTimes / {zip.fail, abort.raise} = {
  let stamped = match field(extra, 0x5455) {
    None -> None,
    Some(data) -> if data.len() < 1 { fail(where, BadExtra) }
    else if data.at(0) & 1 == 0 { None } else {
      match data.u32_at(1) {
        None -> fail(where, BadExtra),
        Some(seconds) ->
          Some(
            Instant(
              (if seconds >= 0x8000_0000 { seconds - 0x1_0000_0000 } else { seconds }) * 1000000000,
            ),
          ),
      }
    },
  };
  match field(extra, 0x000A) {
    None -> { modified: stamped, accessed: None, created: None },
    Some(data) -> {
      let ntfs = ntfs_times(where, data);
      { ..ntfs, modified: if ntfs.modified == None { stamped } else { ntfs.modified } }
    },
  }
}

fn times_field(times: MemberTimes) -> Option<Bytes> = {
  let one_member_archive = if times.accessed == None && times.created == None {
    whole_seconds(times.modified)
  } else { None };
  if times == no_times { Some(b"") } else if one_member_archive != None {
    one_member_archive.map(|seconds: Int|
      field_bytes(0x5455, b"\x01".concat(u32_le(seconds & 0xFFFF_FFFF))))
  } else {
    match ((ticks_of(times.modified), ticks_of(times.accessed), ticks_of(times.created))) {
      (Some(modified), Some(accessed), Some(created)) ->
        Some(
          field_bytes(
            0x000A,
            Bytes::concat_all([
              u32_le(0),
              field_bytes(
                1,
                Bytes::concat_all([u64_le(modified), u64_le(accessed), u64_le(created)]),
              ),
            ]),
          ),
        ),
      _ -> None,
    }
  }
}

/// The limits a reader enforces on an archive. `entries` is the most members. `total` is the most
/// unpacked bytes across all members, and `size` is the most for one member. `ratio` is the largest
/// multiple of a member's stored size that its unpacked size may reach. `path` is the most bytes in
/// a name. `comment` is the most bytes in a member's comment and in the archive's. `slack` is the
/// most bytes of unused space before the first member and after each member.
pub type Limits = new {
  entries: Int,
  total: Int,
  size: Int,
  ratio: Int,
  path: Int,
  comment: Int,
  slack: Int,
}

/// Where an archive's directory is, as its end record states.
pub type Directory = new {
  /// The offset of its first header, and how many bytes its headers are.
  at: Int,
  size: Int,
  entries: Int,
  /// The archive's own comment, in no stated encoding.
  comment: Bytes,
}

/// The result of reading part of an archive: either `Found` with the value, or `NeedMore` asking
/// for more bytes.
pub type Lookup<a> =
  | Found(a)
  /// The bytes given are too few to continue. The number is how many bytes, counted from the end of
  /// the archive, the reader needs.
  | NeedMore(Int)

type EndRecord = { disk: Int, first: Int, disk_entries: Int, entries: Int, size: Int, at: Int }

fn signatures(
  tail: Bytes,
  from: Int,
  accumulator: List<Int>,
  span: Int,
) -> List<Int> / {abort.raise} =
  if span <= 0 { accumulator } else {
    match tail.index_of_from(b"PK\x05\x06", from) {
      Some(p) -> if p + 22 > tail.len() { accumulator }
      else { signatures(tail, p + 1, accumulator.push(p), span - 1) },
      None -> accumulator,
    }
  }

/// Returns where an archive's directory is. `tail` is the last bytes of an archive that is `length`
/// bytes long. Returns `NeedMore(n)` when `tail` is too short, asking for the last `n` bytes of the
/// archive. The first request covers the end record, a comment of up to `limits.comment` bytes and
/// the zip64 locator. If the locator points to a zip64 record earlier in the archive, a second
/// request asks for the bytes from the start of that record. Raises `zip.fail` if the bytes do not
/// end in exactly one end record.
pub fn locate(
  tail: Bytes,
  length: Int,
  limits: Limits,
) -> Lookup<Directory> / {zip.fail, abort.raise} = {
  let have = tail.len();
  let window = min(length, 22 + min(max(limits.comment, 0), 0xFFFF));
  if have > length { fail(length, NoEndRecord) }
  else if have < min(length, window + 20) { NeedMore(min(length, window + 20)) } else {
    let found = signatures(tail, have - window, [], window);
    match found.filter(|p: Int| p + 22 + u16(tail, p + 20) == have) {
      [] -> fail(length, NoEndRecord),
      [p, ..] -> if found.last() != Some(p) { fail(length - have + p, ManyEndRecords) }
      else { from_end_record(tail, length, limits, p) },
    }
  }
}

fn from_end_record(
  tail: Bytes,
  length: Int,
  limits: Limits,
  p: Int,
) -> Lookup<Directory> / {zip.fail, abort.raise} = {
  let base = length - tail.len();
  let where = base + p;
  let narrow: EndRecord = {
    disk: u16(tail, p + 4),
    first: u16(tail, p + 6),
    disk_entries: u16(tail, p + 8),
    entries: u16(tail, p + 10),
    size: Int::of_u32(tail.u32_le(p + 12)),
    at: Int::of_u32(tail.u32_le(p + 16)),
  };
  let comment = tail.slice(p + 22, tail.len());
  if p >= 20 && tail.slice(p - 20, p - 16) == b"PK\x06\x07" {
    match tail.u64_at(p - 12) {
      None -> fail(where, BadNumber),
      Some(record) -> if Int::of_u32(tail.u32_le(p - 16)) != 0
          || Int::of_u32(tail.u32_le(p - 4)) > 1 { fail(where, Split) }
      else if record > where - 76 { fail(where, BadZip64) }
      else if record < base { NeedMore(length - record) }
      else { from_zip64_end_record(tail, base, record, where, narrow, comment, limits) },
    }
  } else if narrow.disk == 0xFFFF || narrow.first == 0xFFFF || narrow.disk_entries == 0xFFFF
      || narrow.entries == 0xFFFF
      || narrow.size == zip64_marker
      || narrow.at == zip64_marker { fail(where, BadZip64) }
  else { directory_of(where, where, narrow, comment, limits) }
}

fn from_zip64_end_record(
  tail: Bytes,
  base: Int,
  record: Int,
  where: Int,
  narrow: EndRecord,
  comment: Bytes,
  limits: Limits,
) -> Lookup<Directory> / {zip.fail, abort.raise} = {
  let r = record - base;
  if tail.slice(r, r + 4) != b"PK\x06\x06"
      || zip64_number(record, tail, r + 4) != where - 32 - record { fail(record, BadZip64) } else {
    let zip64: EndRecord = {
      disk: Int::of_u32(tail.u32_le(r + 16)),
      first: Int::of_u32(tail.u32_le(r + 20)),
      disk_entries: zip64_number(record, tail, r + 24),
      entries: zip64_number(record, tail, r + 32),
      size: zip64_number(record, tail, r + 40),
      at: zip64_number(record, tail, r + 48),
    };
    let same = |short: Int, mark: Int, long: Int| short == mark || short == long;
    if same(narrow.disk, 0xFFFF, zip64.disk) && same(narrow.first, 0xFFFF, zip64.first)
        && same(narrow.disk_entries, 0xFFFF, zip64.disk_entries)
        && same(narrow.entries, 0xFFFF, zip64.entries)
        && same(narrow.size, zip64_marker, zip64.size)
        && same(narrow.at, zip64_marker, zip64.at) {
      directory_of(where, record, zip64, comment, limits)
    } else { fail(where, Disagrees) }
  }
}

fn directory_of(
  where: Int,
  close: Int,
  end: EndRecord,
  comment: Bytes,
  limits: Limits,
) -> Lookup<Directory> / {zip.fail} =
  if end.disk != 0 || end.first != 0 || end.disk_entries != end.entries { fail(where, Split) }
  else if checked_add(end.at, end.size) != Some(close) || end.entries > end.size / 46 {
    fail(where, BadDirectory)
  } else if end.entries > limits.entries { fail(where, TooMany) }
  else { Found({ at: end.at, size: end.size, entries: end.entries, comment: comment }) }

/// How a member's data is stored.
pub type Method =
  /// Stored without compression.
  | Stored
  /// As a DEFLATE stream.
  | Deflated

/// What the directory records for one member, and where its bytes are.
pub type DirectoryEntry = new {
  header: Header,
  /// The member's name exactly as the directory stores it. The header flags are in `flags`, and the
  /// member's own header must contain the same flags. If bit 11 of `flags` is set, the name is
  /// UTF-8. Otherwise its encoding is unknown, so the caller decodes `name` itself.
  name: Bytes,
  flags: Int,
  method: Method,
  /// The CRC-32 of its data.
  crc: U32,
  /// The length of the data in bytes as stored in the archive, and its length after unpacking.
  stored: Int,
  size: Int,
  /// The offset of its own header, and of what follows the member: the next member's header,
  /// or the directory.
  at: Int,
  until: Int,
}

type CentralHeader = {
  made: Int,
  flags: Int,
  method: Int,
  time: Int,
  date: Int,
  crc: Int,
  wide: HeaderNumbers,
  external: Int,
  name: Bytes,
  extra: List<Field>,
  comment: Bytes,
  length: Int,
}

fn read_central_header(
  input: Bytes,
  o: Int,
  where: Int,
) -> CentralHeader / {zip.fail, abort.raise} =
  if input.len() - o < 46 { fail(where, Truncated) }
  else if input.slice(o, o + 4) != b"PK\x01\x02" { fail(where, BadDirectory) } else {
    let n = u16(input, o + 28);
    let m = u16(input, o + 30);
    let k = u16(input, o + 32);
    let name = o + 46;
    if input.len() - name < n + m + k { fail(where, Truncated) } else {
      let extra = fields(input.slice(name + n, name + n + m));
      let twice = [0x0001, 0x000A, 0x5455, 0x7075].any(|id: Int|
        extra.found.count(|f: Field| f.id == id) > 1);
      if !extra.whole || twice { fail(where, BadExtra) } else {
        {
          made: u16(input, o + 4),
          flags: u16(input, o + 8),
          method: u16(input, o + 10),
          time: u16(input, o + 12),
          date: u16(input, o + 14),
          crc: Int::of_u32(input.u32_le(o + 16)),
          wide: with_zip64(
            where,
            extra.found,
            {
              size: Int::of_u32(input.u32_le(o + 24)),
              stored: Int::of_u32(input.u32_le(o + 20)),
              at: Int::of_u32(input.u32_le(o + 42)),
              disk: u16(input, o + 34),
            },
            false,
          ),
          external: Int::of_u32(input.u32_le(o + 38)),
          name: input.slice(name, name + n),
          extra: extra.found,
          comment: input.slice(name + n + m, name + n + m + k),
          length: 46 + n + m + k,
        }
      }
    }
  }

fn unicode_path(r: CentralHeader) -> Option<Bytes> / {abort.raise} =
  match field(r.extra, 0x7075) {
    Some(data) -> if data.u32_at(1) == Some(Int::of_u32(crc32(r.name))) && data.at(0) == 1 {
      Some(data.slice(5, data.len()))
    } else { None },
    None -> None,
  }

fn path_and_kind(
  where: Int,
  r: CentralHeader,
  names: NameEncoding,
) -> { path: String, kind: Kind, mode: Int } / {zip.fail, abort.raise} = {
  let flagged = r.flags & 0x800 != 0;
  let unicode = if flagged { None } else { unicode_path(r) };
  let stored = match unicode { Some(text) -> text, None -> r.name };
  let slashed = stored.ends_with(b"/");
  let spelled = if slashed { stored.slice(0, tar::slash_trimmed_length(stored, stored.len())) }
  else { stored };
  let host = r.made >> 8;
  let unix = host == 3 || host == 19;
  let bits = if unix { r.external >> 28 } else { 0 };
  let kind = if r.name.ends_with(b"/") != slashed { fail(where, BadKind) }
  else if bits == 4 { if slashed { Directory } else { fail(where, BadKind) } }
  else if slashed { if bits == 0 { Directory } else { fail(where, BadKind) } }
  else if bits == 0 || bits == 8 { File }
  else if bits == 10 { Symlink }
  else { Other(bits) };
  {
    path: if flagged || unicode != None { utf8(where, spelled) }
    else { decode_unflagged(spelled, names) },
    kind: kind,
    mode: if unix { r.external >> 16 & 0xFFF }
    else { (if slashed { 493 } else { 420 }) & (if r.external & 1 == 0 { 0xFFF } else { 0x16D }) },
  }
}

fn directory_entry(
  where: Int,
  r: CentralHeader,
  d: Directory,
  limits: Limits,
  names: NameEncoding,
) -> DirectoryEntry / {zip.fail, abort.raise} = {
  let w = r.wide;
  let dense = match checked_mul(w.stored, limits.ratio) {
    Some(most) -> w.size > most,
    None -> false,
  };
  if r.flags & 0x2041 != 0 { fail(where, Encrypted) }
  else if r.method != 0 && r.method != 8 { fail(where, Method(r.method)) }
  else if w.disk != 0 { fail(where, Split) }
  else if r.name.len() > limits.path || r.comment.len() > limits.comment { fail(where, TooLong) }
  else if w.size > limits.size { fail(where, TooLarge) }
  else if dense { fail(where, TooDense) }
  else if r.method == 0 && w.stored != w.size { fail(where, BadNumber) }
  else if w.at > d.at || w.stored > d.at { fail(where, Overlaps) } else {
    let name = path_and_kind(where, r, names);
    if name.kind == Directory && w.size != 0 { fail(where, StrayData) } else {
      {
        header: {
          path: name.path,
          kind: name.kind,
          mode: name.mode,
          modified: from_dos_time(r.date, r.time),
          times: times_of(where, r.extra),
          comment: if r.flags & 0x800 != 0 { utf8(where, r.comment) }
          else { decode_unflagged(r.comment, names) },
          extra: r.extra.filter(|f: Field| !is_known_field(f.id)),
        },
        name: r.name,
        flags: r.flags,
        method: if r.method == 0 { Stored } else { Deflated },
        crc: U32::of_int(r.crc),
        stored: w.stored,
        size: w.size,
        at: w.at,
        until: d.at,
      }
    }
  }
}

type ListingState = {
  at: Int,
  listed: List<DirectoryEntry>,
  names: Map<String, Unit>,
  minimum_sizes: Map<Int, Int>,
  total: Int,
}

fn walk(
  input: Bytes,
  d: Directory,
  limits: Limits,
  names: NameEncoding,
  w: ListingState,
  left: Int,
) -> ListingState / {zip.fail, abort.raise} =
  if left <= 0 { w } else {
    let where = d.at + w.at;
    let r = read_central_header(input, w.at, where);
    let l = directory_entry(where, r, d, limits, names);
    if w.names.contains(l.header.path) { fail(where, Duplicate) }
    else if w.minimum_sizes.contains(l.at) { fail(where, Overlaps) }
    else if l.size > limits.total - w.total { fail(where, TooMuch) } else {
      walk(
        input,
        d,
        limits,
        names,
        {
          at: w.at + r.length,
          listed: w.listed.push(l),
          names: w.names.insert(l.header.path, ()),
          minimum_sizes: w.minimum_sizes.insert(l.at, 30 + r.name.len() + l.stored),
          total: w.total + l.size,
        },
        left - 1,
      )
    }
  }

fn bounded(
  listed: List<DirectoryEntry>,
  least: Map<Int, Int>,
  d: Directory,
  limits: Limits,
) -> List<DirectoryEntry> / {zip.fail} = {
  let starts = least.keys();
  let until = enumerate(starts).fold(
    Map::new(),
    |ends: Map<Int, Int>, s: { index: Int, value: Int }|
      ends.insert(s.value, match starts.at(s.index + 1) { Some(next) -> next, None -> d.at }),
  );
  let first = match starts { [start, ..] -> start, [] -> d.at };
  if first > limits.slack { fail(0, Unclaimed) } else {
    listed.map(|l: DirectoryEntry| {
      let end = match until.get(l.at) { Some(next) -> next, None -> d.at };
      let need = match least.get(l.at) { Some(bytes) -> bytes, None -> 30 };
      if need > end - l.at { fail(l.at, Overlaps) } else { { ..l, until: end } }
    })
  }
}

/// Returns the members a directory lists, in its order. `input` holds the directory's bytes:
/// `d.size` of them, starting at `d.at` in the archive. `names` says how to decode a name whose
/// header does not set the UTF-8 flag. Raises `zip.fail` for an invalid header, a member this
/// module does not read or that exceeds a limit, two members with the same decoded name, and two
/// members whose bytes overlap.
pub fn listing(
  input: Bytes,
  d: Directory,
  limits: Limits,
  names: NameEncoding = Utf8IfValid,
) -> List<DirectoryEntry> / {zip.fail, abort.raise} =
  if input.len() != d.size { fail(d.at, Truncated) }
  else if d.entries > limits.entries { fail(d.at, TooMany) } else {
    let walked = walk(
      input,
      d,
      limits,
      names,
      { at: 0, listed: [], names: Map::new(), minimum_sizes: Map::new(), total: 0 },
      d.entries,
    );
    if walked.at != d.size { fail(d.at + walked.at, BadDirectory) }
    else { bounded(walked.listed, walked.minimum_sizes, d, limits) }
  }

/// A member found: its header, where its data is in the archive and how many bytes it takes
/// there, and what the data must unpack to.
pub type Member = new {
  header: Header,
  /// Its name as the directory writes it, and its header's flags.
  name: Bytes,
  flags: Int,
  method: Method,
  /// The CRC-32 of its data.
  crc: U32,
  /// `data` is the offset of the member's data in the archive, `stored` is its length as stored,
  /// and `size` is its length once unpacked.
  data: Int,
  stored: Int,
  size: Int,
}

fn descriptor_length(l: DirectoryEntry, after: Bytes) -> Option<Int> = {
  let sum = u32_le(Int::of_u32(l.crc));
  let long = Bytes::concat_all([sum, u64_le(l.stored), u64_le(l.size)]);
  let short = Bytes::concat_all([sum, u32_le(l.stored), u32_le(l.size)]);
  let forms = [b"PK\x07\x08".concat(long), long].concat(
    if l.stored >= zip64_marker || l.size >= zip64_marker { [] }
    else { [b"PK\x07\x08".concat(short), short] },
  );
  forms.find(|form: Bytes| after.starts_with(form)).map(|form: Bytes| form.len())
}

fn found_member(
  l: DirectoryEntry,
  limits: Limits,
  data: Int,
  end: Int,
) -> Lookup<Member> / {zip.fail} =
  if l.until - l.at - end > limits.slack { fail(l.at + end, Unclaimed) } else {
    Found({
      header: l.header,
      name: l.name,
      flags: l.flags,
      method: l.method,
      crc: l.crc,
      data: l.at + data,
      stored: l.stored,
      size: l.size,
    })
  }

fn local_member(
  l: DirectoryEntry,
  seen: Bytes,
  limits: Limits,
  data: Int,
) -> Lookup<Member> / {zip.fail, abort.raise} = {
  let n = u16(seen, 26);
  let is_local_header = with_zip64(
    l.at,
    fields(seen.slice(30 + n, data)).found,
    { size: Int::of_u32(seen.u32_le(22)), stored: Int::of_u32(seen.u32_le(18)), at: 0, disk: 0 },
    true,
  );
  let described = l.flags & 8 != 0;
  let same = |here: Int, listed: Int| here == listed || (described && here == 0);
  let method = match l.method { Stored -> 0, Deflated -> 8 };
  if seen.slice(30, 30 + n) != l.name || u16(seen, 6) != l.flags || u16(seen, 8) != method
      || !same(Int::of_u32(seen.u32_le(14)), Int::of_u32(l.crc))
      || !same(is_local_header.stored, l.stored)
      || !same(is_local_header.size, l.size) { fail(l.at, Disagrees) }
  else if !described { found_member(l, limits, data, data + l.stored) } else {
    let end = data + l.stored;
    let need = end + min(l.until - l.at - end, 24);
    if seen.len() < need { NeedMore(need) } else {
      match descriptor_length(l, seen.slice(end, need)) {
        Some(length) -> found_member(l, limits, data, end + length),
        None -> fail(l.at + end, Disagrees),
      }
    }
  }
}

/// Returns the member listed as `l`, with the offset of its data. `seen` is the archive's bytes
/// from `l.at` that have arrived so far, starting with the member's own header. Returns
/// `NeedMore(n)` when `seen` is too short, where `n` is the total length of `seen` needed. That is
/// the length of the header, or, for a member with a data descriptor, the length of the header, the
/// data and the descriptor. Raises `zip.fail` when the header or the descriptor disagrees with the
/// directory, when the member extends into the next member, and when more than `limits.slack` bytes
/// after the member belong to no member.
pub fn open(
  l: DirectoryEntry,
  seen: Bytes,
  limits: Limits,
) -> Lookup<Member> / {zip.fail, abort.raise} =
  if l.until - l.at < 30 { fail(l.at, Overlaps) }
  else if seen.len() < 30 { NeedMore(30) }
  else if seen.slice(0, 4) != b"PK\x03\x04" { fail(l.at, Disagrees) } else {
    let data = 30 + u16(seen, 26) + u16(seen, 28);
    if data > l.until - l.at || l.stored > l.until - l.at - data { fail(l.at, Overlaps) }
    else if seen.len() < data { NeedMore(data) }
    else { local_member(l, seen, limits, data) }
  }

/// A member's data, unpacked: `input` is its `m.stored` bytes from `m.data`. Raises
/// `zip.fail` for data that does not inflate to `m.size` bytes whose CRC-32 is `m.crc`, and
/// for a symbolic link whose target is not UTF-8.
pub fn unpack(m: Member, input: Bytes) -> Bytes / {zip.fail, abort.raise} =
  if input.len() != m.stored { fail(m.data, Truncated) } else {
    let data = match m.method {
      Stored -> input,
      Deflated -> match try_inflate(input, m.size) {
        Ok(inflated) -> inflated,
        Err(_) -> fail(m.data, BadData),
      },
    };
    if data.len() != m.size { fail(m.data, BadData) }
    else if crc32(data) != m.crc { fail(m.data, BadChecksum) }
    else if m.header.kind == Symlink && !data.is_utf8() { fail(m.data, NotUtf8) }
    else { data }
  }

/// Returns every member of `archive`, in its directory's order, with its header and the offset of
/// its data, without unpacking any data. Each member's own header is read and checked against the
/// directory. `names` says how to decode a name whose header does not set the UTF-8 flag.
pub fn members(
  archive: Bytes,
  limits: Limits,
  names: NameEncoding = Utf8IfValid,
) -> List<Member> / {zip.fail, abort.raise} = {
  let length = archive.len();
  match locate(archive, length, limits) {
    NeedMore(_) -> fail(length, Truncated),
    Found(d) ->
      listing(archive.slice(d.at, d.at + d.size), d, limits, names).map(|l: DirectoryEntry|
        match open(l, archive.slice(l.at, l.until), limits) {
          Found(m) -> m,
          NeedMore(_) -> fail(l.at, Truncated),
        }),
  }
}

/// The entries of `archive`. Raises `zip.fail` where `decode` returns `Err`.
pub fn read(
  archive: Bytes,
  limits: Limits,
  names: NameEncoding = Utf8IfValid,
) -> List<Entry> / {zip.fail, abort.raise} =
  members(archive, limits, names).map(|m: Member| -> Entry {
    { header: m.header, data: unpack(m, archive.slice(m.data, m.data + m.stored)) }
  })

/// The entries of `archive`, or why it is not an archive within `limits`. A name with no flag is
/// decoded as `names` directs: as UTF-8 when its bytes are valid UTF-8, unless `Declared` is given.
pub fn decode(
  archive: Bytes,
  limits: Limits,
  names: NameEncoding = Utf8IfValid,
) -> Result<List<Entry>, Error> / {abort.raise} = try { read(archive, limits, names) }

fn unwritable_reason(e: Entry) -> Option<ErrorKind> = {
  let h = e.header;
  let sound = match h.kind {
    Other(bits) -> bits >= 1 && bits <= 15 && bits != 4 && bits != 8 && bits != 10,
    _ -> true,
  };
  let slash = if h.kind == Directory { 1 } else { 0 };
  if h.path == "" || h.path.ends_with("/") || h.path.contains("\0") { Some(BadName) }
  else if Bytes::of_string(h.path).len() + slash > 0xFFFF
      || Bytes::of_string(h.comment).len() > 0xFFFF { Some(TooLong) }
  else if !sound { Some(BadKind) }
  else if h.kind == Directory && e.data.len() > 0 { Some(StrayData) }
  else if h.kind == Symlink && !e.data.is_utf8() { Some(NotUtf8) }
  else if h.mode < 0 || h.mode > 0xFFF { Some(BadNumber) }
  else if dos_time_or_zero(h.modified) == None || times_field(h.times) == None { Some(BadTime) }
  else if h.extra.any(|f: Field| f.id < 0 || f.id > 0xFFFF || is_known_field(f.id))
      || h.extra.fold(0, |sum: Int, f: Field| sum + 4 + f.data.len()) > extra_field_limit {
    Some(BadExtra)
  } else { None }
}

const extra_field_limit: Int = 0xFFFF - 28 - 36

type HeaderFields = {
  name: Bytes,
  comment: Bytes,
  flags: Int,
  method: Int,
  stamp: Int,
  crc: Int,
  stored_size: Int,
  size: Int,
  external: Int,
  extra: Bytes,
  needs: Int,
}

fn header_fields(e: Entry, deflated: Bool, stored: Int) -> HeaderFields = {
  let h = e.header;
  let bits = match h.kind { Directory -> 4, File -> 8, Symlink -> 10, Other(other) -> other };
  {
    name: Bytes::of_string(if h.kind == Directory { h.path ++ "/" } else { h.path }),
    comment: Bytes::of_string(h.comment),
    flags: if h.path.is_ascii() && h.comment.is_ascii() { 0 } else { 0x800 },
    method: if deflated { 8 } else { 0 },
    stamp: match dos_time_or_zero(h.modified) { Some(stamped) -> stamped, None -> 0 },
    crc: Int::of_u32(crc32(e.data)),
    stored_size: stored,
    size: e.data.len(),
    external: (bits << 12 | h.mode) << 16 | (if h.kind == Directory { 0x10 } else { 0 }),
    extra: Bytes::concat_all(
      [match times_field(h.times) { Some(timed) -> timed, None -> b"" }].concat(
        h.extra.map(|f: Field| field_bytes(f.id, f.data)),
      ),
    ),
    needs: if deflated || h.kind == Directory { 20 } else { 10 },
  }
}

fn u32_field(n: Int) -> Bytes = u32_le(min(n, zip64_marker))

fn zip64_field(numbers: List<Int>) -> Bytes =
  if numbers.len() == 0 { b"" }
  else { field_bytes(1, Bytes::concat_all(numbers.map(|n: Int| u64_le(n)))) }

fn local_header(i: HeaderFields) -> Bytes = {
  let zip64_number = i.stored_size >= zip64_marker || i.size >= zip64_marker;
  let extra = zip64_field(if zip64_number { [i.size, i.stored_size] } else { [] }).concat(i.extra);
  Bytes::concat_all([
    b"PK\x03\x04",
    u16_le(if zip64_number { 45 } else { i.needs }),
    u16_le(i.flags),
    u16_le(i.method),
    u16_le(i.stamp & 0xFFFF),
    u16_le(i.stamp >> 16),
    u32_le(i.crc),
    if zip64_number { u32_le(zip64_marker) } else { u32_le(i.stored_size) },
    if zip64_number { u32_le(zip64_marker) } else { u32_le(i.size) },
    u16_le(i.name.len()),
    u16_le(extra.len()),
    i.name,
    extra,
  ])
}

fn central_header(i: HeaderFields, at: Int) -> Bytes = {
  let numbers = [i.size, i.stored_size, at].filter(|n: Int| n >= zip64_marker);
  let extra = zip64_field(numbers).concat(i.extra);
  let needs = if numbers.len() == 0 { i.needs } else { 45 };
  Bytes::concat_all([
    b"PK\x01\x02",
    u16_le(0x0300 | needs),
    u16_le(needs),
    u16_le(i.flags),
    u16_le(i.method),
    u16_le(i.stamp & 0xFFFF),
    u16_le(i.stamp >> 16),
    u32_le(i.crc),
    u32_field(i.stored_size),
    u32_field(i.size),
    u16_le(i.name.len()),
    u16_le(extra.len()),
    u16_le(i.comment.len()),
    b"\x00\x00\x00\x00",
    u32_le(i.external),
    u32_field(at),
    i.name,
    extra,
    i.comment,
  ])
}

/// An archive being written: the members' bytes not yet returned by `flush`, the offset the next
/// member starts at, the directory's header for each member so far, and their names.
pub type Writer = new {
  pending: List<Bytes>,
  at: Int,
  directory: List<Bytes>,
  names: Map<String, Unit>,
}

/// A writer with nothing written.
pub const begin: Writer = { pending: [], at: 0, directory: [], names: Map::new() }

/// Returns `w` with entry `e` added after the members already added. The data is deflated if that
/// makes it smaller, and stored unchanged otherwise. Raises `zip.write_failed` for an entry no
/// header can store, and for a second entry with the same name.
pub fn add(w: Writer, e: Entry) -> Writer / {zip.write_failed, abort.raise} = {
  let index = w.directory.len();
  match unwritable_reason(e) {
    Some(reason) -> fail_write(index, reason),
    None -> if w.names.contains(e.header.path) { fail_write(index, Duplicate) } else {
      let packed = deflate(e.data);
      let deflated = packed.len() < e.data.len();
      let stored = if deflated { packed } else { e.data };
      let written = header_fields(e, deflated, stored.len());
      let is_local_header = local_header(written);
      {
        pending: w.pending.push(is_local_header).push(stored),
        at: w.at + is_local_header.len() + stored.len(),
        directory: w.directory.push(central_header(written, w.at)),
        names: w.names.insert(e.header.path, ()),
      }
    },
  }
}

/// What `flush` returns: the bytes written since the last flush, and the writer with none pending.
pub type FlushResult = new { bytes: Bytes, writer: Writer }

/// Returns the bytes of the members added since the last flush, and `w` with nothing pending. A
/// caller can append these bytes to a file between calls to `add`, so that only one member is in
/// memory at a time.
pub fn flush(w: Writer) -> FlushResult =
  { bytes: Bytes::concat_all(w.pending), writer: { ..w, pending: [] } }

fn end_records(entries: Int, size: Int, at: Int, comment: Bytes) -> Bytes =
  Bytes::concat_all([
    if entries < 0xFFFF && size < zip64_marker && at < zip64_marker { b"" } else {
      Bytes::concat_all([
        b"PK\x06\x06",
        u64_le(44),
        u16_le(0x032D),
        u16_le(45),
        u32_le(0),
        u32_le(0),
        u64_le(entries),
        u64_le(entries),
        u64_le(size),
        u64_le(at),
        b"PK\x06\x07",
        u32_le(0),
        u64_le(at + size),
        u32_le(1),
      ])
    },
    b"PK\x05\x06\x00\x00\x00\x00",
    u16_le(min(entries, 0xFFFF)),
    u16_le(min(entries, 0xFFFF)),
    u32_field(size),
    u32_field(at),
    u16_le(comment.len()),
    comment,
  ])

fn locates_directory(
  tail: Bytes,
  length: Int,
  at: Int,
  size: Int,
  entries: Int,
) -> Bool / {abort.raise} = {
  let found = try {
    locate(tail, length, {
      entries: entries,
      total: 0,
      size: 0,
      ratio: 0,
      path: 0,
      comment: 0xFFFF,
      slack: 0,
    })
  };
  match found {
    Ok(Found(d)) -> d.at == at && d.size == size && d.entries == entries,
    Ok(NeedMore(_)) -> true,
    Err(_) -> false,
  }
}

/// Returns the bytes not yet returned by `flush`, followed by the directory and the end record with
/// `comment`. If `flush` was never called, this is the whole archive. Raises `zip.write_failed` in
/// two cases: when `comment` is longer than 65535 bytes, and when `comment` or the last member
/// contains bytes a reader would take for another end record. The `index` of the error is the
/// number of entries written.
pub fn finish(w: Writer, comment: Bytes = b"") -> Bytes / {zip.write_failed, abort.raise} = {
  let entries = w.directory.len();
  let directory = Bytes::concat_all(w.directory);
  let size = directory.len();
  if comment.len() > 0xFFFF { fail_write(entries, TooLong) } else {
    let end = end_records(entries, size, w.at, comment);
    let archive_bytes = Bytes::concat_all(w.pending.concat([directory, end]));
    if locates_directory(archive_bytes, w.at + size + end.len(), w.at, size, entries) {
      archive_bytes
    } else { fail_write(entries, ManyEndRecords) }
  }
}

/// The archive of `entries`, in their order. Raises `zip.write_failed` where `encode` returns
/// `Err`. The same entries always produce the same bytes.
pub fn write(entries: List<Entry>) -> Bytes / {zip.write_failed, abort.raise} =
  finish(entries.fold(begin, add))

/// The archive of `entries`, or the first of them that cannot be written and why.
pub fn encode(entries: List<Entry>) -> Result<Bytes, WriteError> / {abort.raise} =
  try { write(entries) }

fn has_drive_letter(segment: String) -> Bool = {
  let raw = Bytes::of_string(segment);
  raw.len() >= 2 && raw.at(1) == b':'
    && (match raw.at(0) { b'a'..=b'z' -> true, b'A'..=b'Z' -> true, _ -> false })
}

fn windows_danger(name: String) -> Option<UnsafeReason> =
  if name.contains("\\") { Some(Backslash) }
  else if name.split("/").any(has_drive_letter) { Some(Drive) }
  else { None }

fn target(e: Entry) -> String / {abort.raise} =
  if e.header.kind == Symlink && e.data.is_utf8() { String::of_bytes(e.data) } else { "" }

fn entry_danger(e: Entry) -> Option<UnsafeReason> / {abort.raise} =
  match windows_danger(e.header.path) {
    Some(reason) -> Some(reason),
    None -> windows_danger(target(e)),
  }

fn tar_entry(e: Entry) -> tar::Entry / {abort.raise} = {
  let h = e.header;
  match h.kind {
    File -> tar::file(h.path, e.data, mode: h.mode),
    Directory -> tar::directory(h.path, mode: h.mode),
    Symlink -> tar::symlink(h.path, target(e)),
    Other(_) -> {
      let made = tar::file(h.path, b"");
      { ..made, header: { ..made.header, kind: tar::Fifo } }
    },
  }
}

/// The operations that extract `entries`, in order, beneath a root. Raises `tar.unsafe` where
/// `plan` returns `Err`.
pub fn extraction(entries: List<Entry>) -> List<Operation> / {tar.unsafe, abort.raise} =
  match enumerate(entries).find(|e: { index: Int, value: Entry }| entry_danger(e.value) != None) {
    None -> tar::plan_or_raise(entries.map(tar_entry)),
    Some(first) -> {
      let before = tar::plan_or_raise(entries.take(first.index).map(tar_entry));
      match entry_danger(first.value) {
        Some(reason) ->
          tar.unsafe({ index: first.index, path: first.value.header.path, reason: reason }),
        None -> before,
      }
    },
  }

/// Returns the operations that extract `entries`, in order, beneath a root, or the first entry that
/// is unsafe to extract and why. An entry is unsafe if `std.tar` rejects it, or if its path or
/// symbolic link target contains a backslash or a drive letter.
pub fn plan(entries: List<Entry>) -> Result<List<Operation>, UnsafeEntry> / {abort.raise} =
  try { extraction(entries) }

/// The path beneath a root that `path` names, as `std.tar`'s `safe_path` returns it, with `Err`
/// also for a path with a backslash or a drive letter.
pub fn safe_path(path: String) -> Result<String, UnsafeReason> =
  match windows_danger(path) { Some(reason) -> Err(reason), None -> tar::safe_path(path) }

const large_limits: Limits =
  {
    entries: 100000,
    total: 100000000,
    size: 100000000,
    ratio: 2000,
    path: 4096,
    comment: 65535,
    slack: 0,
  }

const sample_paths: List<String> =
  [
    "a",
    "dir/file.txt",
    "caf\u{e9}/\u{6c34}.txt",
    "name with spaces",
    String::join(range(0, 30).map(|_: Int| "0123456789ab"), "/"),
    "a/b/c/d",
    "\u{3b1}\u{3b2}",
    "x.y",
  ]

fn spelled(pick: Int) -> String =
  match sample_paths.at(pick & 7) { Some(path) -> path, None -> "a" }

fn drawn_date_time(pick: Int) -> DateTime =
  DateTime(Date(3652 + pick % 46751), Time(pick / 46751 % 43200 * 2000000000))

fn random_instant(pick: Int) -> Option<Instant> =
  if pick & 3 == 0 { None }
  else if pick & 3 == 1 { Some(Instant((pick >> 2 & 0x7FFF_FFFF) * 1000000000)) }
  else { Some(Instant((pick >> 2 & 0xFFFF_FFFF_FFFF) * 100)) }

fn random_entry(index: Int, drawn_pick: Int, data: Bytes) -> Entry = {
  let pick = drawn_pick *% 0x2545_F491_4F6C_DD1D;
  let path = spelled(pick) ++ "-" ++ index.to_string();
  let kind = pick >> 3 & 3;
  let made = if kind == 0 { directory(path) }
  else if kind == 1 { symlink(path, spelled(pick >> 5)) }
  else if kind == 2 { file(path, Bytes::concat_all([data, data, data, data])) }
  else { file(path, data) };
  {
    header: {
      ..made.header,
      mode: pick >> 8 & 0xFFF,
      modified: if pick & 0x10_0000 == 0 { None }
      else { Some(drawn_date_time(pick >> 21 & 0x7FFF_FFFF)) },
      times: {
        modified: random_instant(pick >> 22),
        accessed: random_instant(pick >> 30 & 0xFFFF_FFFE),
        created: random_instant(pick >> 30 & 0xFFFF_FFFE),
      },
      comment: if pick & 0x20_0000 == 0 { "" } else { spelled(pick >> 24) },
      extra: if pick & 0x40_0000 == 0 { [] } else { [{ id: 0xCAFE, data: data }] },
    },
    data: made.data,
  }
}

fn random_entries(picks: List<Int>, data: Bytes) -> List<Entry> =
  enumerate(picks).map(|p: { index: Int, value: Int }| random_entry(p.index, p.value, data))

test "written archive entries decode back to the same entries, with both the default and `Declared` name encodings" {
  gen::check2(gen::list(gen::any_int()), gen::bytes(), |picks: List<Int>, data: Bytes| {
    let entries = random_entries(picks, data);
    let whole = write(entries);
    decode(whole, large_limits) == Ok(entries)
      && decode(whole, large_limits, Declared) == Ok(entries)
  })
}

test "`locate` on the last bytes of an archive finds the directory, or reports how many total bytes it needs" {
  gen::check3(
    gen::list(gen::any_int()),
    gen::bytes(),
    gen::any_int(),
    |picks: List<Int>, data: Bytes, cut: Int| {
      let whole = write(random_entries(picks, data));
      let length = whole.len();
      let tight = { ..large_limits, comment: 0 };
      let tail = whole.slice(length - (cut & 0x7FFF_FFFF) % (length + 1), length);
      match try { locate(tail, length, tight) } {
        Ok(NeedMore(needed)) -> needed > tail.len() && needed <= length,
        Ok(found) -> (try { locate(whole, length, tight) }) == Ok(found),
        Err(_) -> false,
      }
    },
  )
}

fn sample_segments(text: String) -> List<String> =
  ["a", "..", ".", "", "caf\u{e9}", "\u{6c34}\u{df}", "c:", text]

fn utf8_as_code_page_437(text: String) -> String / {abort.raise} =
  of_code_page_437(Bytes::of_string(text))

test "`safe_path` accepts or rejects a path the same way whether it is read as UTF-8 or as code page 437" {
  gen::check2(gen::list(gen::any_int()), gen::text(), |picks: List<Int>, text: String| {
    let path = String::join(
      picks.map(|pick: Int|
        match sample_segments(text).at(pick & 7) { Some(name) -> name, None -> "a" }),
      "/",
    );
    match safe_path(path) {
      Ok(safe) -> safe_path(utf8_as_code_page_437(path)) == Ok(utf8_as_code_page_437(safe)),
      Err(reason) -> safe_path(utf8_as_code_page_437(path)) == Err(reason),
    }
  })
}

fn patched(bytes: Bytes, at: Int, patch: Bytes) -> Bytes / {abort.raise} =
  Bytes::concat_all([bytes.slice(0, at), patch, bytes.slice(at + patch.len(), bytes.len())])

fn read_error(archive: Bytes, limits: Limits) -> Option<Error> / {abort.raise} =
  match decode(archive, limits) { Ok(_) -> None, Err(rejected) -> Some(rejected) }

fn pair() -> Bytes / {zip.write_failed, abort.raise} = write([file("a", b"x"), file("b", b"y")])

fn one_member_archive(
  listed: HeaderFields,
  is_local_header: HeaderFields,
  stored: Bytes,
  after: Bytes,
) -> Bytes = {
  let body = Bytes::concat_all([local_header(is_local_header), stored, after]);
  let zip64 = central_header(listed, 0);
  Bytes::concat_all([body, zip64, end_records(1, zip64.len(), body.len(), b"")])
}

type RawMember = { image: HeaderFields, data: Bytes }

fn empty_member(i: HeaderFields) -> RawMember = { image: i, data: b"" }

fn raw_archive(members: List<RawMember>) -> Bytes = {
  let put = members.fold(
    { body: [], directory: [], at: 0 },
    |so_far: { body: List<Bytes>, directory: List<Bytes>, at: Int }, m: RawMember| {
      let is_local_header = local_header(m.image);
      {
        body: so_far.body.push(is_local_header).push(m.data),
        directory: so_far.directory.push(central_header(m.image, so_far.at)),
        at: so_far.at + is_local_header.len() + m.data.len(),
      }
    },
  );
  let directory = Bytes::concat_all(put.directory);
  Bytes::concat_all(
    put.body.concat([directory, end_records(members.len(), directory.len(), put.at, b"")]),
  )
}

fn unflagged_archive(entries: List<Entry>) -> Bytes =
  raw_archive(
    entries.map(|e: Entry| -> RawMember {
      { image: { ..header_fields(e, false, e.data.len()), flags: 0 }, data: e.data }
    }),
  )

test "an archive of one file is its header, its data, the directory's header and an end record" {
  let archive = write([file("hello.txt", b"hi\n")]);
  assert_eq(archive.len(), 119);
  let zip64 = b"\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x21\x00\x7a\x7a\x6f\xed\x03\x00\x00\x00\x03\x00\x00\x00";
  assert_eq(
    archive.slice(0, 30),
    Bytes::concat_all([b"PK\x03\x04\x0a\x00", zip64, b"\x09\x00\x00\x00"]),
  );
  assert_eq(archive.slice(30, 42), b"hello.txthi\n");
  assert_eq(
    archive.slice(42, 88),
    Bytes::concat_all([
      b"PK\x01\x02\x0a\x03\x0a\x00",
      zip64,
      b"\x09\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\xa4\x81\x00\x00\x00\x00",
    ]),
  );
  assert_eq(archive.slice(88, 97), b"hello.txt");
  assert_eq(
    archive.slice(97, 119),
    b"PK\x05\x06\x00\x00\x00\x00\x01\x00\x01\x00\x37\x00\x00\x00\x2a\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00",
  );
  assert_eq(read(archive, large_limits), [file("hello.txt", b"hi\n")]);
  assert_eq(write([]), b"PK\x05\x06".concat(b"\x00".repeat(18)));
  assert_eq(read(write([]), large_limits), [])
}

test "entry constructors default to Unix modes, no timestamps and the earliest date a header can store" {
  let made = file("a", b"x");
  assert_eq(made.header, {
    path: "a",
    kind: File,
    mode: 420,
    modified: date::date_time(1980, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0),
    times: { modified: None, accessed: None, created: None },
    comment: "",
    extra: [],
  });
  let noon = DateTime(Date(19724), Time(43200000000000));
  assert_eq(file("a", b"x", mode: 493, modified: noon).header, {
    ..made.header,
    mode: 493,
    modified: date::date_time(2024, 1, 2, 12, 0, 0),
  });
  assert_eq(directory("d").header, { ..made.header, path: "d", kind: Directory, mode: 493 });
  assert_eq(symlink("l", "d/x"), {
    header: { ..made.header, path: "l", kind: Symlink, mode: 511 },
    data: b"d/x",
  });
  assert_eq(write([made, directory("d")]), write([file("a", b"x"), directory("d")]))
}

test "a file is deflated where that is smaller, and stored where it is not" {
  let lines = b"the same line, again and again\n".repeat(40);
  let archive = write([file("lines", lines), file("word", b"abc"), file("none", b"")]);
  assert_eq(
    members(archive, large_limits).map(|m: Member| (m.method, m.stored, m.size, m.data)),
    [(Deflated, 39, 1240, 35), (Stored, 3, 3, 108), (Stored, 0, 0, 145)],
  );
  assert_eq(read(archive, large_limits).map(|e: Entry| e.data), [lines, b"abc", b""])
}

test "zip header DOS dates and times convert to and from calendar times in two-second steps from 1980 to 2107, and invalid values return `None`" {
  assert_eq(from_dos_time(0x5822, 0x5883), date::date_time(2024, 1, 2, 11, 4, 6));
  assert_eq(from_dos_time(0x0021, 0), date::date_time(1980, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0));
  assert_eq(from_dos_time(0xFF9F, 0xBF7D), date::date_time(2107, 12, 31, 23, 59, 58));
  assert_eq(
    [(0, 0), (0x0020, 0), (0x0001, 0), (0x01A1, 0), (0x0021, 0xC000), (0x0021, 0x07C0)].map(
      |f: (Int, Int)| from_dos_time(f._0, f._1),
    ),
    [None, None, None, None, None, None],
  );
  assert_eq(from_dos_time(0x0021, 0x001E), None);
  assert_eq(from_dos_time(0x005D, 0), date::date_time(1980, 2, 29, 0, 0, 0));
  assert_eq(from_dos_time(0x025D, 0), None);
  assert_eq(date::date_time(2024, 1, 2, 11, 4, 6).map(to_dos_time), Some(Some(0x5822_5883)));
  assert_eq(
    [
      date::date_time(1979, 12, 31, 23, 59, 58),
      date::date_time(2108, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0),
      date::date_time(2024, 1, 2, 11, 4, 5),
    ].map(|at: Option<DateTime>|
      match at { Some(clocked) -> to_dos_time(clocked), None -> Some(-1) }),
    [None, None, None],
  );
  assert_eq(to_dos_time(DateTime(Date(19724), Time(1))), None);
  let undated = file("a", b"");
  let unset = { ..undated, header: { ..undated.header, modified: None } };
  assert_eq(write([unset]).slice(10, 14), b"\x00\x00\x00\x00");
  assert_eq(read(write([unset]), large_limits), [unset])
}

test "a zip file name is decoded as code page 437 unless its UTF-8 flag is set, and a valid extra field with a UTF-8 name replaces it" {
  assert_eq(
    of_code_page_437(Bytes::concat_all(range(128, 256).map(|byte: Int| byte_of_int(byte)))),
    "ÇüéâäàåçêëèïîìÄÅÉæÆôöòûùÿÖÜ¢£¥₧ƒ"
      ++ "áíóúñÑªº¿⌐¬½¼¡«»░▒▓│┤╡╢╖╕╣║╗╝╜╛┐"
      ++ "└┴┬├─┼╞╟╚╔╩╦╠═╬╧╨╤╥╙╘╒╓╫╪┘┌█▄▌▐▀"
      ++ "αßΓπΣσµτΦΘΩδ∞φε∩≡±≥≤⌠⌡÷≈°∙·√ⁿ²■\u{a0}",
  );
  assert_eq(of_code_page_437(b"plain\x00\x1f\x7f"), "plain\0\u{1f}\u{7f}");
  let plainly = header_fields(file("caf_.txt", b""), false, 0);
  let paths = |i: HeaderFields|
    read(one_member_archive(i, i, b"", b""), large_limits).map(|e: Entry| e.header.path);
  assert_eq(paths({ ..plainly, name: b"caf\x82.txt" }), ["caf\u{e9}.txt"]);
  assert_eq(paths({ ..plainly, name: b"caf\xc3\xa9.txt", flags: 0x800 }), ["caf\u{e9}.txt"]);
  assert_eq(
    read_error(
      one_member_archive({ ..plainly, name: b"caf\x82.txt", flags: 0x800 }, plainly, b"", b""),
      large_limits,
    ),
    Some({ at: 38, reason: NotUtf8 }),
  );
  let unicode = |sum: Int, text: Bytes|
    { ..plainly, extra: field_bytes(0x7075, Bytes::concat_all([b"\x01", u32_le(sum), text])) };
  let sum = Int::of_u32(crc32(b"caf_.txt"));
  assert_eq(paths(unicode(sum, b"caf\xc3\xa9.txt")), ["caf\u{e9}.txt"]);
  assert_eq(paths(unicode(sum + 1, b"caf\xc3\xa9.txt")), ["caf_.txt"]);
  assert_eq(
    read(one_member_archive(unicode(sum, b"x"), plainly, b"", b""), large_limits).map(|e: Entry|
      e.header.extra),
    [[]],
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(one_member_archive(unicode(sum, b"caf\x82.txt"), plainly, b"", b""), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 38, reason: NotUtf8 }),
  );
  let noted = file("a", b"");
  let flags = |e: Entry| members(write([e]), large_limits).map(|m: Member| (m.flags, m.name));
  assert_eq(flags(file("caf\u{e9}", b"")), [(0x800, b"caf\xc3\xa9")]);
  assert_eq(flags(noted), [(0, b"a")]);
  assert_eq(flags({ ..noted, header: { ..noted.header, comment: "\u{e9}" } }), [(0x800, b"a")])
}

test "a zip name without the UTF-8 flag is read as UTF-8 when it is valid UTF-8, or as code page 437 when the caller passes `Declared`" {
  let plainly = header_fields(file("a", b""), false, 0);
  let read_names = |i: HeaderFields, names: NameEncoding|
    read(raw_archive([empty_member(i)]), large_limits, names).map(|e: Entry|
      (e.header.path, e.header.comment));
  let both = |i: HeaderFields| (read_names(i, Utf8IfValid), read_names(i, Declared));
  let accented = { ..plainly, name: b"caf\xc3\xa9-\xe6\xb0\xb4.txt", comment: b"na\xc3\xafve" };
  assert_eq(both(accented), (
    [("caf\u{e9}-\u{6c34}.txt", "na\u{ef}ve")],
    [("caf\u{251c}\u{2310}-\u{b5}\u{2591}\u{2524}.txt", "na\u{251c}\u{bb}ve")],
  ));
  assert_eq(
    read(raw_archive([empty_member(accented)]), large_limits).map(|e: Entry| e.header.path),
    ["caf\u{e9}-\u{6c34}.txt"],
  );
  assert_eq(
    members(raw_archive([empty_member(accented)]), large_limits).map(|m: Member| (m.flags, m.name)),
    [(0, b"caf\xc3\xa9-\xe6\xb0\xb4.txt")],
  );
  assert_eq(both({ ..plainly, name: b"caf\x82", comment: b"\xc3\xa9" }), (
    [("caf\u{e9}", "\u{e9}")],
    [("caf\u{e9}", "\u{251c}\u{2310}")],
  ));
  assert_eq(both({ ..plainly, name: b"plain", comment: b"too" }), (
    [("plain", "too")],
    [("plain", "too")],
  ));
  assert_eq(
    [b"a\xc0\xafb", b"\xc0\xae\xc0\xae", b"\xe0\x80\xaf", b"\xed\xa0\x80", b"\xf4\x90\x80\x80"].map(
      |name: Bytes| both({ ..plainly, name: name }),
    ),
    [
      "a\u{2514}\u{bb}b",
      "\u{2514}\u{ab}\u{2514}\u{ab}",
      "\u{3b1}\u{c7}\u{bb}",
      "\u{3c6}\u{e1}\u{c7}",
      "\u{2320}\u{c9}\u{c7}\u{c7}",
    ].map(|path: String| ([(path, "")], [(path, "")])),
  );
  let twice = raw_archive([
    empty_member({ ..plainly, name: b"\xc3\xa9" }),
    empty_member({ ..plainly, name: b"\x82" }),
  ]);
  assert_eq(read_error(twice, large_limits), Some({ at: 111, reason: Duplicate }));
  assert_eq(read(twice, large_limits, Declared).map(|e: Entry| e.header.path), [
    "\u{251c}\u{2310}",
    "\u{e9}",
  ]);
  let written = write([file("caf\u{e9}", b"")]);
  assert_eq(read(written, large_limits, Declared), read(written, large_limits, Utf8IfValid))
}

test "extracting a zip archive decides whether two names are the same letters after decoding, so the encoding changes which names collide" {
  let through = unflagged_archive([symlink("\u{c9}", "x"), file("\u{e9}/planted", b"")]);
  assert_eq(
    plan(read(through, large_limits)),
    Err({ index: 1, path: "\u{e9}/planted", reason: ThroughLink }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    plan(read(through, large_limits, Declared)),
    Ok([
      MakeSymlink("\u{251c}\u{eb}", "x"),
      MakeDirectory("\u{251c}\u{2310}", 493),
      WriteFile("\u{251c}\u{2310}/planted", 420, b""),
    ]),
  );
  let apart = unflagged_archive([symlink("\u{c0}", "x"), file("\u{c7}/planted", b"")]);
  assert_eq(
    plan(read(apart, large_limits)),
    Ok([
      MakeSymlink("\u{c0}", "x"),
      MakeDirectory("\u{c7}", 493),
      WriteFile("\u{c7}/planted", 420, b""),
    ]),
  );
  assert_eq(
    plan(read(apart, large_limits, Declared)),
    Err({ index: 1, path: "\u{251c}\u{e7}/planted", reason: ThroughLink }),
  )
}

test "a reader enforces the entry, size, ratio and length limits it was given" {
  let noted = file("two/long-name", b"123");
  let archive = write([
    file("one", b"12345"),
    { ..noted, header: { ..noted.header, comment: "noted" } },
    directory("d"),
  ]);
  let tight = { ..large_limits, entries: 3, total: 8, size: 5, path: 13, comment: 5 };
  assert_eq(read_error(archive, tight), None);
  assert_eq(read_error(archive, { ..tight, entries: 2 }), Some({ at: 277, reason: TooMany }));
  assert_eq(read_error(archive, { ..tight, size: 4 }), Some({ at: 116, reason: TooLarge }));
  assert_eq(read_error(archive, { ..tight, total: 7 }), Some({ at: 165, reason: TooMuch }));
  assert_eq(read_error(archive, { ..tight, path: 12 }), Some({ at: 165, reason: TooLong }));
  assert_eq(read_error(archive, { ..tight, comment: 4 }), Some({ at: 165, reason: TooLong }));
  let dense = write([file("r", b"the same line, again and again\n".repeat(40))]);
  assert_eq(read_error(dense, { ..large_limits, ratio: 32 }), None);
  assert_eq(read_error(dense, { ..large_limits, ratio: 31 }), Some({ at: 70, reason: TooDense }));
  assert_eq(read_error(dense, { ..large_limits, size: 1239 }), Some({ at: 70, reason: TooLarge }));
  assert_eq(read_error(archive, { ..tight, ratio: 1 }), None)
}

test "a zip archive must end with its end record, so extra or missing bytes fail, and a comment longer than the comment limit fails" {
  let archive = pair();
  assert_eq(archive.len(), 180);
  assert_eq(read_error(archive.concat(b"!"), large_limits), Some({ at: 181, reason: NoEndRecord }));
  assert_eq(
    read_error(archive.slice(0, 179), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 179, reason: NoEndRecord }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(archive.slice(0, 100), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 100, reason: NoEndRecord }),
  );
  assert_eq(read_error(b"", large_limits), Some({ at: 0, reason: NoEndRecord }));
  let commented = finish(add(begin, file("a", b"x")), comment: b"a comment");
  assert_eq(read(commented, large_limits).map(|e: Entry| e.header.path), ["a"]);
  assert_eq(
    match try { locate(commented, commented.len(), large_limits) } {
      Ok(Found(d)) -> d.comment,
      _ -> b"",
    },
    b"a comment",
  );
  assert_eq(read_error(commented, { ..large_limits, comment: 9 }), None);
  assert_eq(
    read_error(commented, { ..large_limits, comment: 8 }),
    Some({ at: 110, reason: NoEndRecord }),
  );
  let fake = b"PK\x05\x06".concat(b"\x00".repeat(18));
  let doubled = patched(archive, 178, b"\x16").concat(fake);
  assert_eq(read_error(doubled, large_limits), Some({ at: 158, reason: ManyEndRecords }));
  let short = Bytes::concat_all([patched(archive, 178, b"\x17"), fake, b"!"]);
  assert_eq(read_error(short, large_limits), Some({ at: 158, reason: ManyEndRecords }));
  assert_eq(read_error(patched(archive, 178, b"\x04").concat(b"PK\x05\x06"), large_limits), None);
  let descriptor_length = |comment: Bytes|
    try { finish(add(begin, file("a", b"x")), comment: comment) };
  assert_eq(descriptor_length(fake), Err({ index: 1, reason: ManyEndRecords }));
  assert_eq(descriptor_length(b"c".repeat(65536)), Err({ index: 1, reason: TooLong }));
  assert_eq(
    descriptor_length(b"c".repeat(65535)).ok().map(|whole: Bytes| whole.len()),
    Some(65636),
  );
  let planted = |reach: Bytes|
    file("p", Bytes::concat_all([b"PK\x05\x06", b"0123456789abcdef", reach]));
  assert_eq(encode([planted(b"\x45\x00")]), Err({ index: 1, reason: ManyEndRecords }));
  let near = write([planted(b"\x44\x00")]);
  assert_eq(read(near, large_limits), [planted(b"\x44\x00")]);
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(near, 51, b"\x45"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 31, reason: ManyEndRecords }),
  );
  let noted = file("a", b"x");
  let locator = String::of_bytes(b"PK\x06\x07".concat(b"\x00".repeat(16)));
  let pointing = [{ ..noted, header: { ..noted.header, comment: locator } }];
  assert_eq(encode(pointing), Err({ index: 1, reason: ManyEndRecords }));
  assert_eq(encode(pointing.concat([file("b", b"")])).is_ok(), true)
}

test "a zip central directory must contain exactly the headers its end record counts, at the position the end record gives" {
  let archive = pair();
  let counted = |count: Bytes| patched(patched(archive, 166, count), 168, count);
  assert_eq(read_error(counted(b"\x01"), large_limits), Some({ at: 111, reason: BadDirectory }));
  assert_eq(read_error(counted(b"\x03"), large_limits), Some({ at: 158, reason: BadDirectory }));
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(archive, 166, b"\x01"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 158, reason: Split }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(archive, 162, b"\x01"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 158, reason: Split }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(archive, 174, b"\x41"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 158, reason: BadDirectory }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(archive, 170, b"\x5d"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 158, reason: BadDirectory }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(archive, 64, b"XK"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 64, reason: BadDirectory }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(archive, 139, b"\x09"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 111, reason: Truncated }),
  );
  assert_eq(read_error(counted(b"\xff\xff"), large_limits), Some({ at: 158, reason: BadZip64 }));
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(archive, 84, b"\xff\xff\xff\xff"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 64, reason: BadZip64 }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(b"stub".concat(archive), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 162, reason: BadDirectory }),
  )
}

test "zip members must have different names, must not overlap each other's bytes, and must use a compression method that is supported" {
  let archive = pair();
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(archive, 157, b"a"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 111, reason: Duplicate }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(archive, 153, b"\x00"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 111, reason: Overlaps }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(patched(archive, 84, b"\x28"), 88, b"\x28"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 0, reason: Overlaps }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(archive, 153, b"\x21"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 33, reason: Overlaps }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(archive, 153, b"\x41"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 111, reason: Overlaps }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(archive, 26, b"\x02"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 0, reason: Overlaps }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(archive, 74, b"\x0c"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 64, reason: Method(12) }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(archive, 74, b"\x09"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 64, reason: Method(9) }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    [b"\x01\x00", b"\x40\x00", b"\x00\x20"].map(|flags: Bytes|
      read_error(patched(archive, 72, flags), large_limits)),
    range(0, 3).map(|_: Int| Some({ at: 64, reason: Encrypted })),
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(archive, 88, b"\x02"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 64, reason: BadNumber }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(archive, 98, b"\x01"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 64, reason: Split }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(archive, 94, b"\x03"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 64, reason: BadExtra }),
  )
}

test "a member's own header agrees with what the directory records for it" {
  let archive = pair();
  assert_eq(
    [
      patched(archive, 30, b"c"),
      patched(archive, 7, b"\x08"),
      patched(archive, 8, b"\x08"),
      patched(archive, 14, b"\x00"),
      patched(archive, 18, b"\x00"),
      patched(archive, 22, b"\x00"),
      patched(archive, 0, b"PK\x01\x02"),
      patched(archive, 32, b"PK\x07\x08"),
    ].map(|changed: Bytes| read_error(changed, large_limits)),
    List::concat(range(0, 7).map(|_: Int| Some({ at: 0, reason: Disagrees })), [
      Some({ at: 32, reason: Disagrees }),
    ]),
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(archive, 31, b"z"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 31, reason: BadChecksum }),
  );
  assert_eq(read_error(patched(archive, 4, b"\x2d\x00"), large_limits), None);
  assert_eq(read_error(patched(archive, 10, b"\x83\x58\x22\x58"), large_limits), None);
  let deflated = write([file("r", b"abcabcabc".repeat(20))]);
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(deflated, 33, b"\xff"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 31, reason: BadData }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    read_error(
      patched(patched(deflated, deflated.len() - 22 - 47 + 24, b"\xb5"), 22, b"\xb5"),
      large_limits,
    ),
    Some({ at: 31, reason: BadData }),
  )
}

test "a data descriptor is read in each of its forms, and must match the directory" {
  let listed = { ..header_fields(file("a", b"x"), false, 1), flags: 8 };
  let is_local_header = { ..listed, crc: 0, stored_size: 0, size: 0 };
  let sum = u32_le(listed.crc);
  let short = Bytes::concat_all([sum, u32_le(1), u32_le(1)]);
  let long = Bytes::concat_all([sum, u64_le(1), u64_le(1)]);
  let reads = |after: Bytes|
    decode(one_member_archive(listed, is_local_header, b"x", after), large_limits);
  assert_eq(
    [short, long, b"PK\x07\x08".concat(short), b"PK\x07\x08".concat(long)].map(reads),
    range(0, 4).map(|_: Int| Ok([file("a", b"x")])),
  );
  assert_eq(
    decode(one_member_archive(listed, listed, b"x", short), large_limits),
    Ok([file("a", b"x")]),
  );
  assert_eq(reads(b"").err(), Some({ at: 32, reason: Disagrees }));
  assert_eq(reads(patched(short, 0, b"\x00")).err(), Some({ at: 32, reason: Disagrees }));
  assert_eq(reads(patched(short, 4, b"\x02")).err(), Some({ at: 32, reason: Disagrees }));
  assert_eq(reads(short.slice(0, 11)).err(), Some({ at: 32, reason: Disagrees }));
  assert_eq(
    decode(
      one_member_archive(listed, { ..is_local_header, size: 2 }, b"x", short),
      large_limits,
    ).err(
    ),
    Some({ at: 0, reason: Disagrees }),
  );
  assert_eq(reads(short.concat(b"!")).err(), Some({ at: 44, reason: Unclaimed }));
  assert_eq(
    decode(one_member_archive(listed, is_local_header, b"x", short.concat(b"!")), {
      ..large_limits,
      slack: 1,
    }).is_ok(
    ),
    true,
  );
  let decode_unflagged = header_fields(file("a", b"x"), false, 1);
  assert_eq(
    decode(one_member_archive(decode_unflagged, decode_unflagged, b"x", short), large_limits).err(),
    Some({ at: 32, reason: Unclaimed }),
  )
}

test "reading a zip archive fails on bytes before, between or after its members, unless `slack` allows that many bytes" {
  let entries = [file("a", b"x"), file("b", b"y")];
  let behind = finish(entries.fold({ ..begin, at: 4 }, add));
  let stubbed = b"stub".concat(behind);
  assert_eq(read_error(stubbed, large_limits), Some({ at: 0, reason: Unclaimed }));
  assert_eq(read_error(stubbed, { ..large_limits, slack: 3 }), Some({ at: 0, reason: Unclaimed }));
  assert_eq(decode(stubbed, { ..large_limits, slack: 4 }), Ok(entries));
  let first = add(begin, file("a", b"x"));
  let gapped = finish(
    add({ ..first, pending: first.pending.push(b"gap"), at: first.at + 3 }, file("b", b"y")),
  );
  assert_eq(read_error(gapped, large_limits), Some({ at: 32, reason: Unclaimed }));
  assert_eq(decode(gapped, { ..large_limits, slack: 3 }), Ok(entries));
  let last = add(first, file("b", b"y"));
  let blocked = finish({
    ..last,
    pending: last.pending.push(b"a signing block"),
    at: last.at + 15,
  });
  assert_eq(read_error(blocked, large_limits), Some({ at: 64, reason: Unclaimed }));
  assert_eq(decode(blocked, { ..large_limits, slack: 15 }), Ok(entries));
  assert_eq(
    read_error(b"stub".concat(finish({ ..begin, at: 4 })), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 0, reason: Unclaimed }),
  )
}

test "an archive is read a step at a time from the bytes each step requests" {
  let archive = write([file("first", b"0123456789"), file("second", b"abc")]);
  let length = archive.len();
  let tight = { ..large_limits, comment: 0 };
  assert_eq(locate(b"", length, tight), NeedMore(42));
  assert_eq(locate(archive.slice(length - 41, length), length, tight), NeedMore(42));
  assert_eq(locate(b"", length, large_limits), NeedMore(length));
  let d = match locate(archive.slice(length - 42, length), length, tight) {
    Found(directory) -> directory,
    NeedMore(_) -> panic("an end record and a locator are forty-two bytes"),
  };
  assert_eq(d, { at: 84, size: 103, entries: 2, comment: b"" });
  let listed = listing(archive.slice(84, 187), d, tight);
  assert_eq(
    listed.map(|l: DirectoryEntry| (l.header.path, l.at, l.until, l.stored, l.size, l.method)),
    [("first", 0, 45, 10, 10, Stored), ("second", 45, 84, 3, 3, Stored)],
  );
  assert_eq(
    Result::err(try { listing(archive.slice(84, 186), d, tight) }),
    Some({ at: 84, reason: Truncated }),
  );
  let second_entry_error = match listed { [_, l] -> l, _ -> panic("two members are listed") };
  let seen = |n: Int| open(second_entry_error, archive.slice(45, 45 + n), tight);
  assert_eq((seen(0), seen(29), seen(30), seen(35)), (
    NeedMore(30),
    NeedMore(30),
    NeedMore(36),
    NeedMore(36),
  ));
  let m = match seen(36) { Found(member) -> member, NeedMore(_) -> panic("a header is a member") };
  assert_eq((m.data, m.stored, m.size, m.header.path), (81, 3, 3, "second"));
  assert_eq(seen(39), Found(m));
  assert_eq(unpack(m, archive.slice(81, 84)), b"abc");
  assert_eq(
    Result::err(try { unpack(m, archive.slice(81, 83)) }),
    Some({ at: 81, reason: Truncated }),
  )
}

test "a number too large for a zip header field is written in a zip64 extra field, and a number that fits is not written there" {
  let small = header_fields(file("a", b""), false, 0);
  let far = 5000000000;
  let widths = |i: HeaderFields, at: Int| {
    let zip64 = central_header(i, at);
    (zip64.len() - 47, zip64.slice(20, 28), zip64.slice(42, 46), u16(zip64, 6))
  };
  let ones = b"\xff\xff\xff\xff";
  assert_eq(widths(small, 7), (0, b"\x00".repeat(8), b"\x07\x00\x00\x00", 10));
  assert_eq(widths(small, 0xFFFF_FFFE), (0, b"\x00".repeat(8), b"\xfe\xff\xff\xff", 10));
  assert_eq(widths(small, 0xFFFF_FFFF), (12, b"\x00".repeat(8), ones, 45));
  assert_eq(widths({ ..small, size: far }, 7), (
    12,
    b"\x00\x00\x00\x00".concat(ones),
    b"\x07\x00\x00\x00",
    45,
  ));
  assert_eq(widths({ ..small, size: far, stored_size: far }, far), (
    28,
    ones.concat(ones),
    ones,
    45,
  ));
  assert_eq(
    read_central_header(
      central_header({ ..small, size: far, stored_size: far + 1 }, far + 2),
      0,
      0,
    ).wide,
    { size: far, stored: far + 1, at: far + 2, disk: 0 },
  );
  assert_eq(read_central_header(central_header({ ..small, stored_size: far }, 3), 0, 0).wide, {
    size: 0,
    stored: far,
    at: 3,
    disk: 0,
  });
  let is_local_header = local_header({ ..small, stored_size: far });
  assert_eq(is_local_header.len(), 31 + 20);
  assert_eq((u16(is_local_header, 4), is_local_header.slice(18, 26)), (45, ones.concat(ones)));
  assert_eq(
    with_zip64(
      0,
      fields(is_local_header.slice(31, 51)).found,
      { size: zip64_marker, stored: zip64_marker, at: 0, disk: 0 },
      true,
    ),
    { size: 0, stored: far, at: 0, disk: 0 },
  );
  let field_of = |data: Bytes| fields(field_bytes(1, data)).found;
  let marked = { size: zip64_marker, stored: 1, at: zip64_marker, disk: 0 };
  assert_eq(
    try { with_zip64(9, field_of(u64_le(1).concat(b"\xff".repeat(8))), marked, false) },
    Err({ at: 9, reason: BadNumber }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    try { with_zip64(9, field_of(u64_le(1).concat(b"\x00".repeat(7))), marked, false) },
    Err({ at: 9, reason: BadZip64 }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    try { with_zip64(9, field_of(u64_le(1).concat(u64_le(2))), marked, false) },
    Ok({ size: 1, stored: 1, at: 2, disk: 0 }),
  )
}

test "a zip end record is read with its zip64 end record only when its fields say so, and fails when the two records' numbers differ" {
  let far = 5000000000;
  let end = end_records(70000, 3220000, far, b"zip64");
  let length = far + 3220000 + end.len();
  let loose = { ..large_limits, comment: 5 };
  assert_eq(end.len(), 56 + 20 + 22 + 5);
  assert_eq(locate(end.slice(76, 103), length, loose), NeedMore(47));
  assert_eq(locate(end.slice(56, 103), length, loose), NeedMore(103));
  assert_eq(locate(end.slice(1, 103), length, loose), NeedMore(103));
  assert_eq(
    locate(end, length, loose),
    Found({ at: far, size: 3220000, entries: 70000, comment: b"zip64" }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    try { locate(end, length, { ..loose, entries: 69999 }) },
    Err({ at: length - 27, reason: TooMany }),
  );
  let rejected = |changed: Bytes| Result::err(try { locate(changed, length, loose) });
  assert_eq(
    rejected(patched(end, 76 + 12, b"\x00\x00\x00\x00")),
    Some({ at: length - 27, reason: Disagrees }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    rejected(patched(end, 76 + 8, b"\x70\x11")),
    Some({ at: length - 27, reason: Disagrees }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    locate(patched(end, 76 + 16, b"\xff\xff\xff\xff"), length, loose),
    locate(end, length, loose),
  );
  assert_eq(
    locate(patched(end, 76 + 12, b"\xff\xff\xff\xff"), length, loose),
    locate(end, length, loose),
  );
  assert_eq(rejected(patched(end, 0, b"XK")), Some({ at: length - 103, reason: BadZip64 }));
  assert_eq(rejected(patched(end, 4, b"\x2d")), Some({ at: length - 103, reason: BadZip64 }));
  assert_eq(rejected(patched(end, 64, b"\x21")), Some({ at: length - 27, reason: BadZip64 }));
  assert_eq(locate(patched(end, 64, b"\x1f"), length, loose), NeedMore(104));
  assert_eq(
    rejected(b"P".concat(patched(patched(end, 64, b"\x1f"), 0, b"K\x06\x06\x2c"))),
    Some({ at: length - 104, reason: BadZip64 }),
  );
  assert_eq(rejected(patched(end, 60, b"\x01")), Some({ at: length - 27, reason: Split }));
  assert_eq(rejected(patched(end, 72, b"\x02")), Some({ at: length - 27, reason: Split }));
  assert_eq(rejected(patched(end, 16, b"\x01")), Some({ at: length - 27, reason: Disagrees }));
  assert_eq(
    rejected(patched(patched(end, 16, b"\x01"), 80, b"\x01")),
    Some({ at: length - 27, reason: Split }),
  );
  assert_eq(rejected(patched(end, 48, b"\x01")), Some({ at: length - 27, reason: BadDirectory }));
  assert_eq(rejected(patched(end, 55, b"\x80")), Some({ at: length - 103, reason: BadNumber }));
  assert_eq(end_records(65534, 0xFFFF_FFFE, 0xFFFF_FFFE, b"").len(), 22);
  assert_eq(
    [
      end_records(65535, 0, 0, b""),
      end_records(0, 0xFFFF_FFFF, 0, b""),
      end_records(0, 0, 0xFFFF_FFFF, b""),
    ].map(|records: Bytes| records.len()),
    [98, 98, 98],
  )
}

test "a Unix mode gives a zip member's kind and permissions, and members from other systems get them from the member's name and attributes" {
  let archive = pair();
  let kinds = |changed: Bytes|
    read(changed, large_limits).map(|e: Entry| (e.header.kind, e.header.mode));
  assert_eq(kinds(archive), [(File, 420), (File, 420)]);
  assert_eq(kinds(patched(archive, 104, b"\xff\x1f")), [(Other(1), 4095), (File, 420)]);
  assert_eq(kinds(patched(archive, 104, b"\x6d\x01")), [(File, 365), (File, 420)]);
  assert_eq(kinds(patched(archive, 105, b"\xa1")), [(Symlink, 420), (File, 420)]);
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(archive, 105, b"\x41"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 64, reason: BadKind }),
  );
  let windows_danger = patched(patched(archive, 69, b"\x00"), 104, b"\xff\xff");
  assert_eq(kinds(windows_danger), [(File, 420), (File, 420)]);
  assert_eq(kinds(patched(windows_danger, 102, b"\x01")), [(File, 292), (File, 420)]);
  let folder = write([directory("d"), symlink("l", "d")]);
  assert_eq(kinds(folder), [(Directory, 493), (Symlink, 511)]);
  assert_eq(kinds(patched(folder, 69, b"\x00")), [(Directory, 493), (Symlink, 511)]);
  assert_eq(kinds(patched(patched(folder, 69, b"\x00"), 102, b"\x11")), [
    (Directory, 365),
    (Symlink, 511),
  ]);
  assert_eq(
    read_error(patched(folder, 105, b"\x81"), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 64, reason: BadKind }),
  );
  let filled = {
    ..header_fields(directory("d"), false, 1),
    size: 1,
    crc: Int::of_u32(crc32(b"x")),
  };
  assert_eq(
    read_error(one_member_archive(filled, filled, b"x", b""), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 33, reason: StrayData }),
  );
  let link = header_fields(symlink("l", "t"), false, 2);
  let badly = { ..link, size: 2, crc: Int::of_u32(crc32(b"\xff\xfe")) };
  assert_eq(
    read_error(one_member_archive(badly, badly, b"\xff\xfe", b""), large_limits),
    Some({ at: 31, reason: NotUtf8 }),
  )
}

test "a zip extra field stores a member's times, and extra fields this module does not read are copied unchanged" {
  let second_entry_error = Instant(1704164645000000000);
  let alone_modified: MemberTimes = {
    modified: Some(second_entry_error),
    accessed: None,
    created: None,
  };
  assert_eq(times_field(no_times), Some(b""));
  assert_eq(times_field(alone_modified), Some(b"UT\x05\x00\x01\x25\x7d\x93\x65"));
  assert_eq(
    times_field({ modified: Some(Instant(-1000000000)), accessed: None, created: None }),
    Some(b"UT\x05\x00\x01\xff\xff\xff\xff"),
  );
  let fine: MemberTimes = {
    modified: Some(Instant(1704164645123456700)),
    accessed: Some(second_entry_error),
    created: None,
  };
  let early: MemberTimes = { ..alone_modified, modified: Some(Instant(50)) };
  let late: MemberTimes = { ..alone_modified, modified: Some(Instant(2147483648000000000)) };
  assert_eq(
    times_field(fine),
    Some(
      Bytes::concat_all([
        b"\x0a\x00\x20\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x01\x00\x18\x00",
        u64_le(133486382451234567),
        u64_le(133486382450000000),
        u64_le(0),
      ]),
    ),
  );
  assert_eq(times_field(early), None);
  assert_eq(times_field(late).map(|input: Bytes| input.len()), Some(36));
  let plainly = file("a", b"");
  let timed = |times: MemberTimes| { ..plainly, header: { ..plainly.header, times: times } };
  assert_eq(read(write([timed(alone_modified)]), large_limits), [timed(alone_modified)]);
  assert_eq(read(write([timed(fine)]), large_limits), [timed(fine)]);
  assert_eq(encode([timed(early)]), Err({ index: 0, reason: BadTime }));
  assert_eq(read(write([timed(late)]), large_limits), [timed(late)]);
  let both = |fields: List<Bytes>| {
    let i = { ..header_fields(plainly, false, 0), extra: Bytes::concat_all(fields) };
    read(one_member_archive(i, i, b"", b""), large_limits).map(|e: Entry|
      (e.header.times, e.header.extra))
  };
  let stamp_field = field_bytes(0x5455, b"\x03\x25\x7d\x93\x65");
  let ntfs_field = |modified: Int|
    field_bytes(
      0x000A,
      Bytes::concat_all([
        u32_le(0),
        field_bytes(1, Bytes::concat_all([u64_le(modified), u64_le(0), u64_le(0)])),
      ]),
    );
  let owner = field_bytes(0x7875, b"\x01\x04\xe8\x03\x00\x00\x04\x64\x00\x00\x00");
  assert_eq(both([stamp_field, owner]), [
    (alone_modified, [{ id: 0x7875, data: b"\x01\x04\xe8\x03\x00\x00\x04\x64\x00\x00\x00" }]),
  ]);
  let finer: MemberTimes = { ..alone_modified, modified: Some(Instant(1704164645123456700)) };
  assert_eq(both([stamp_field, ntfs_field(133486382451234567)]), [(finer, [])]);
  assert_eq(both([stamp_field, ntfs_field(0)]), [(alone_modified, [])]);
  assert_eq(both([field_bytes(0x5455, b"\x02")]), [(no_times, [])]);
  let rejected = |fields: List<Bytes>| {
    let i = { ..header_fields(plainly, false, 0), extra: Bytes::concat_all(fields) };
    read_error(one_member_archive(i, i, b"", b""), large_limits).map(|r: Error|
      (r.at - i.extra.len(), r.reason))
  };
  assert_eq(
    [
      [field_bytes(0x5455, b"")],
      [field_bytes(0x5455, b"\x01\x00\x00\x00")],
      [stamp_field, stamp_field],
      [field_bytes(0x000A, b"\x00\x00")],
      [field_bytes(0x000A, u32_le(0).concat(field_bytes(1, b"short")))],
      [b"\x55\x54\x09\x00\x01"],
      [b"x"],
    ].map(
      rejected,
    ),
    range(0, 7).map(|_: Int| Some((31, BadExtra))),
  )
}

fn second_entry_error(reason: ErrorKind) -> Result<Bytes, WriteError> =
  Err({ index: 1, reason: reason })

test "an entry no header can store is reported with its index and why" {
  let plain = file("a", b"");
  let with_header = |h: Header| encode([plain, { header: h, data: b"" }]);
  assert_eq(["", "a/", "a\0b"].map(|path: String| with_header({ ..plain.header, path: path })), [
    second_entry_error(BadName),
    second_entry_error(BadName),
    second_entry_error(BadName),
  ]);
  assert_eq(with_header(plain.header), second_entry_error(Duplicate));
  let long = |n: Int, kind: Kind|
    with_header({ ..plain.header, path: String::of_bytes(b"n".repeat(n)), kind: kind });
  assert_eq((long(65536, File), long(65535, Directory)), (
    second_entry_error(TooLong),
    second_entry_error(TooLong),
  ));
  assert_eq((long(65535, File).is_ok(), long(65534, Directory).is_ok()), (true, true));
  assert_eq(
    with_header({ ..plain.header, path: "b", comment: String::of_bytes(b"c".repeat(65536)) }),
    second_entry_error(TooLong),
  );
  assert_eq(
    [0, 4, 8, 10, 16, -1].map(|bits: Int|
      with_header({ ..plain.header, path: "b", kind: Other(bits) })),
    range(0, 6).map(|_: Int| second_entry_error(BadKind)),
  );
  assert_eq(
    decode(
      write([
        { ..plain, header: { ..plain.header, kind: Other(1) } },
        { ..plain, header: { ..plain.header, path: "s", kind: Other(12), mode: 0 } },
      ]),
      large_limits,
    ),
    Ok([
      { ..plain, header: { ..plain.header, kind: Other(1) } },
      { ..plain, header: { ..plain.header, path: "s", kind: Other(12), mode: 0 } },
    ]),
  );
  assert_eq(
    encode([{ header: directory("d").header, data: b"x" }]),
    Err({ index: 0, reason: StrayData }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    encode([{ header: symlink("l", "t").header, data: b"\xff" }]),
    Err({ index: 0, reason: NotUtf8 }),
  );
  assert_eq(with_header({ ..plain.header, path: "b", mode: 4096 }), second_entry_error(BadNumber));
  assert_eq(with_header({ ..plain.header, path: "b", mode: -1 }), second_entry_error(BadNumber));
  assert_eq(
    with_header({ ..plain.header, path: "b", modified: date::date_time(1979, 12, 31, 0, 0, 0) }),
    second_entry_error(BadTime),
  );
  assert_eq(
    with_header({ ..plain.header, path: "b", modified: date::date_time(2024, 1, 2, 0, 0, 1) }),
    second_entry_error(BadTime),
  );
  let with_field = |id: Int, data: Bytes|
    with_header({ ..plain.header, path: "b", extra: [{ id: id, data: data }] });
  assert_eq(
    [0x0001, 0x000A, 0x5455, 0x7075, 0x10000, -1].map(|id: Int| with_field(id, b"")),
    range(0, 6).map(|_: Int| second_entry_error(BadExtra)),
  );
  assert_eq(with_field(0xCAFE, b"x".repeat(65468)), second_entry_error(BadExtra));
  let roomiest = with_field(0xCAFE, b"x".repeat(65467));
  assert_eq(roomiest.ok().map(|whole: Bytes| read(whole, large_limits).len()), Some(2))
}

test "a writer that flushes each member as it is added writes the same archive" {
  let entries = [directory("d"), file("d/lines", b"line\n".repeat(50)), symlink("l", "d/lines")];
  let streamed = entries.fold(
    { bytes: b"", writer: begin },
    |so_far: { bytes: Bytes, writer: Writer }, e: Entry| {
      let flushed = flush(add(so_far.writer, e));
      { bytes: so_far.bytes.concat(flushed.bytes), writer: flushed.writer }
    },
  );
  assert_eq(flush(streamed.writer).bytes, b"");
  assert_eq(streamed.bytes.concat(finish(streamed.writer)), write(entries));
  assert_eq(flush(entries.fold(begin, add)).bytes, streamed.bytes)
}

test "zip extraction steps follow `std.tar`: they set modes, create parent directories, and reject paths that escape or pass through a symlink" {
  assert_eq(
    plan([
      file("a/b/one.txt", b"1", mode: 2541),
      directory("a", mode: 448),
      symlink("a/b/link", "one.txt"),
      file("a/b/one.txt", b"2"),
    ]),
    Ok([
      MakeDirectory("a", 493),
      MakeDirectory("a/b", 493),
      WriteFile("a/b/one.txt", 493, b"1"),
      MakeDirectory("a", 448),
      MakeSymlink("a/b/link", "one.txt"),
      WriteFile("a/b/one.txt", 420, b"2"),
    ]),
  );
  assert_eq(
    plan([file("ok", b""), file("../x", b"")]),
    Err({ index: 1, path: "../x", reason: Escapes }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    plan([file("/etc/passwd", b"")]),
    Err({ index: 0, path: "/etc/passwd", reason: Absolute }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    plan([symlink("way", "elsewhere"), file("way/planted", b"")]),
    Err({ index: 1, path: "way/planted", reason: ThroughLink }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    plan([directory("d"), symlink("d/l", "../..")]),
    Err({ index: 1, path: "d/l", reason: LinkEscapes }),
  );
  let plain = file("node", b"");
  assert_eq(
    plan([{ ..plain, header: { ..plain.header, kind: Other(1) } }]),
    Err({ index: 0, path: "node", reason: Special }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    plan([{ header: symlink("l", "t").header, data: b"\xff" }]),
    Err({ index: 0, path: "l", reason: NoName }),
  )
}

test "zip extraction steps reject a backslash or a drive letter in a path or in a symlink target" {
  let after = |path: String| plan([file("ok", b""), file(path, b"")]);
  let rejected = |path: String|
    after(path).err().map(|u: UnsafeEntry| (u.index, u.path == path, u.reason));
  assert_eq(
    ["..\\x", "a\\b", "C:\\x"].map(rejected),
    range(0, 3).map(|_: Int| Some((1, true, Backslash))),
  );
  assert_eq(["C:x", "c:/x", "z:"].map(rejected), range(0, 3).map(|_: Int| Some((1, true, Drive))));
  assert_eq(after("ab:c"), Ok([WriteFile("ok", 420, b""), WriteFile("ab:c", 420, b"")]));
  assert_eq(after("1:c"), Ok([WriteFile("ok", 420, b""), WriteFile("1:c", 420, b"")]));
  assert_eq(
    ["a/../C:x", "./c:", "d/C:"].map(rejected),
    range(0, 3).map(|_: Int| Some((1, true, Drive))),
  );
  assert_eq(
    after("d/1:"),
    Ok([WriteFile("ok", 420, b""), MakeDirectory("d", 493), WriteFile("d/1:", 420, b"")]),
  );
  assert_eq(["a\\b", "D:", "x/D:y"].map(|target: String| plan([symlink("l", target)]).err()), [
    Some({ index: 0, path: "l", reason: Backslash }),
    Some({ index: 0, path: "l", reason: Drive }),
    Some({ index: 0, path: "l", reason: Drive }),
  ]);
  assert_eq(
    plan([file("../a", b""), file("b\\c", b"")]),
    Err({ index: 0, path: "../a", reason: Escapes }),
  );
  assert_eq(
    plan([file("b\\c", b""), file("../a", b"")]),
    Err({ index: 0, path: "b\\c", reason: Backslash }),
  );
  assert_eq(["a/b", "./a//b/", "a/../b", "a\\b", "C:b", "../b", "/b"].map(safe_path), [
    Ok("a/b"),
    Ok("a/b"),
    Ok("b"),
    Err(Backslash),
    Err(Drive),
    Err(Escapes),
    Err(Absolute),
  ])
}

test "`std.tar`'s `extract` carries out zip extraction steps inside a target directory, creating its files, directories and symlinks" {
  let steps = match plan([
      directory("d", mode: 448),
      file("d/f.txt", b"data"),
      symlink("l", "d/f.txt"),
    ]) {
    Ok(tar_entry) -> tar_entry,
    Err(_) -> panic("planning failed for entries that the test expects to be safe to extract"),
  };
  let over = fs::on_mem[d](fs::mem_create_dir(fs::mem_empty(), "out").fs, ||
    tar::extract[d](steps, "out"));
  assert_eq(over.value, [tar::Extracted, tar::Extracted, tar::Extracted]);
  assert_eq(fs::mem_read(over.tree, "out/d/f.txt"), Some(b"data"));
  assert_eq(fs::mem_read_link(over.tree, "out/l"), Some("d/f.txt"));
  assert_eq(fs::mem_mode(over.tree, "out/d"), Some(fs::mode_of_bits(448)))
}
